window.CET4_BANKS.push({"id": "2025-06-2", "source": "2025年06月真题·第2套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: Suppose your university is conducting a survey to collect students' opinions on the appropriate use of AI technology in assisting learning. You are now to write an essay to express your view. You will have 30 minutes for the task. You should write at least 120 words but no more than 180 words.", "sample": "In my view, AI technology, when used appropriately, is a valuable ally in learning rather than a shortcut that\nreplaces genuine effort. Since our university is surveying students on this issue, I would like to voice my\nsupport for embracing AI in a sensible, balanced way.\nThere are solid reasons for this position. First, AI tools raise learning efficiency: a chatbot can explain a\nconfusing grammar point instantly, check my essay for mistakes, or gather background material that once took\nhours in the library. Second, AI offers personalized tutoring, adjusting its explanations to my own pace and\nweak spots. Admittedly, however, over-reliance is a real danger; if students let AI think for them, their own\nanalytical skills will gradually wither.\nTherefore, the key lies in \"appropriate\" use. We should treat AI as an assistant that guides and inspires, not a\nmachine that hands us ready-made answers. Used within this boundary, AI can truly make learning smarter."}, "translation": {"prompt": "近年来，中国东北地区正在大力开发冰雪资源。例如，哈尔滨利用丰富的冰雪资源打造了极具地方特色的\"冰雪大世界\"，让游客在欣赏冰雪之美的同时也能体验当地独特的民俗文化。如今，曾令人畏惧的冰天雪地正吸引着四面八方的游客，成为深受欢迎的旅游胜地。冰雪旅游业正为当地的经济发展做出越来越大的贡献。", "sample": "In recent years, Northeast China has been vigorously developing its ice and snow resources. For example, Harbin has built the Ice and Snow World with strong local characteristics by making full use of its rich ice and snow resources, allowing tourists to experience the unique local folk culture while enjoying the beauty of ice and snow. Today, the once-feared land of ice and snow is attracting tourists from all directions and has become a popular tourist destination. Ice and snow tourism is making an increasingly great contribution to the local economic development."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet4/paper/2025-06-2/?f=w", "clozePassage": "Sir Alfred Hitchcock was an English movie director and producer. Famously  __26__  to as 'The Master of Suspense,' Hitchcock is one of the most influential filmmakers in the history of  __27__  . He was an extraordinary director who entertained his audience with  __28__  and gripping suspense movies.\nHis fascination with crime began at an early age when he was  __29__  by his father who made him spend a few minutes inside a prison for his misconduct. Hence, his movies are  __30__  of the guilt and innocence of the wrongdoer and the victim. He had a gift for creating stories that consisted of deceit, robbery, murder, corruption, and other criminal  __31__  with incredible plot twists in the storyline. The principal characters in his movies were often ordinary people  __32__  in unwanted and unavoidable situations.\nHe was  __33__  productive and his amazing body of work is considered fascinating by the critics. Most of his movies have  __34__  the test of time and are considered to be masterpieces. He is also remembered by other filmmakers, for he continues to inspire them to be passionate about their work. He is also  __35__  by people around the world for his entertaining and exciting stories.", "matchIntro": "Is juicing actually good for you?", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "Freshly juiced fruit has become a basic dietary item—especially for busy, health-conscious consumers who can save time on making (and perhaps even chewing) their food without missing out on nutrients. Fruit juice also has been linked to claims that it can help you both lose weight and stay healthy. All of which, of course, has made it highly profitable. The global fruit and vegetable juices market was valued at $154 billion in 2016 and is expected to grow."}, {"label": "B", "text": "But is juicing really as healthy as we think? Most foods containing fructose—a naturally occurring sugar found in all fruits and fruit juices—don't seem to have harmful effects as long as they're not contributing to excess daily calories. This is because the fibre found in whole fruits is intact, and the sugar is contained within the fruit's cells. It takes our digestive system a while to break these cells down and for fructose to enter the bloodstream."}, {"label": "C", "text": "But this is not the case with fruit juice. Unlike whole fruit, fruit juice has most of the fibre removed. This is why the fructose in fruit juices counts as 'free sugars'—which also include honey and the sugars added to foods. It's recommended by the World Health Organisation that adults have no more than 30g of added sugar, the equivalent of 150ml of fruit juice, per day. The problem is that, with the fibre removed, fruit juice's fructose is absorbed more quickly. Sudden increases in blood sugar cause the release of insulin(胰岛素) to bring it back down to a stable level. Over time, this mechanism can wear out, increasing the risk of developing type 2 diabetes. In 2013, researchers analysed the health data of 100,000 people collected between 1986 and 2009 and found that fruit juice consumption was linked with increased risk of type 2 diabetes. Another study found a relationship between fruit juice and type 2 diabetes after following the diets and diabetes status of more than 70,000 nurses over 18 years. The researchers explain that the possible reason for this may partly have been the lack of the other components found in whole fruit, like fibre."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Juices containing vegetables can provide more nutrients and less sugar than juices made up of just fruit—but they still lack valuable fibre. Diets high in fibre have been linked to a lower risk of developing heart disease, stroke, high blood pressure and diabetes, and it's recommended adults consume 30g per day."}, {"label": "E", "text": "Aside from the link to type 2 diabetes, many studies agree that fruit juice is harmful if it contributes to an excess in calories. In an analysis of 155 studies, John Sievenpiper, associate professor at the University of Toronto's Department of Nutritional Sciences, set out to understand if the associations between sugary soft drinks and health—including risk of diabetes and heart disease—were applicable to the food and drink we generally consume as part of a healthy diet. He compared studies looking into the effects of fructose-containing sugars with control diets free from or lower in these sugars. His aim? To isolate the effects of consuming too many calories from the effects of foods containing different sugars."}, {"label": "F", "text": "He found negative effects on blood sugar and insulin levels when food provided excess calories from sugars, including fruit juice. However, when the calories weren't in excess overall, there were some advantages to consuming whole fruit—and even fruit juice. Sievenpiper concludes that the recommended 150ml of fruit juice per day, which is an average serving, is reasonable. The paper concluded that foods containing fructose may have some small benefits for long-term blood sugar control when they don't lead to over-consumption of calories. But when they do lead to too many calories, they risk raising blood sugar and insulin levels."}, {"label": "G", "text": "So while we know that fruit juice can cause diabetes if it's part of a diet that has more calories overall, it's less clear how juice affects the long-term health of those who aren't overweight. But we have a higher risk of consuming more than the recommended daily number of calories (around 2,000 for women and 2,500 for men) on the days we drink juice, according to research. Numerous studies have shown that drinking fruit juice doesn't make us consume any less of other foods throughout the day."}, {"label": "H", "text": "As it is easy to consume large amounts of fruit juice quite quickly, extra calories are taken in. And extra calories can contribute to weight gain. One way we can improve the effects of fruit juice on our health is making sure we select ripe fruit to retain as much of its goodness as possible, according to Roger Clemens, professor of pharmaceutical(药物的) sciences at the University of Southern California."}, {"label": "I", "text": "Another reason for fruit juice's recent popularity is the argument that it can help to remove poisonous or harmful substances from the body. However, \"the whole concept of a juice diet helping to remove poisonous or harmful substances from the body is completely false,\" says Clemens. \"We consume compounds every day that may be poisonous and our bodies do a wonderful job of removing and eliminating anything harmful from our bodies.\""}, {"label": "J", "text": "And juice isn't a cure-all for getting your nutrients, either. \"There are a lot of nutrients contained in the parts of the fruits, such as in apple skins, that are excluded when you juice them,\" says Heather Ferris, a professor of medicine. \"You end up with sugary water with some vitamins.\""}, {"label": "K", "text": "Plus, she adds, it's not an ideal way to reach the advised 'five a day'. \"People try to get five servings of fruit and vegetables per day and don't realise this isn't just about getting vitamins,\" she says. \"It's also about reducing the amount of grain carbs(碳水化合物) and protein and fats in our diet, and increasing fibre.\" So while fruit juice is better than no fruit at all, there are limits. It particularly comes with risks when we consume more than 150ml of free sugars per day, or when it puts us over our recommended calorie intake. It provides us with vitamins—but fruit juice is far from a quick fix."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "We all take a little extra effort to look nice for special occasions. But most of us have conflicting feelings about dressing up and feel guilty about taking the time to focus on clothes. Science now suggests the right dress may give ourselves the extra edge in our professional and personal lives.\nWe hear sayings like \"dress for the job you want; not the job you have\". Most people don't really believe in them, but research into the impact of clothes on behavior now suggests that there may actually be a grain of truth in these sayings. Science says that the clothes we wear affect our behavior, our mood and even the way we interact with others because of the symbolic meaning that we assign to different types of clothing.\nWe consider some clothes to be powerful, some to be fun, and so on. We even evaluate people whom we have just met based on their clothes. We also evaluate ourselves based on what we are wearing because of the way they make us feel. This means that the experience of wearing something affects our attitudes and our choice of behavior.\nThere's a reason tailored jackets are associated with being 'dressed for success'. It seems that wearing formal office wear puts us in the right frame of mind to conduct business. Wearing power clothing makes us feel more confident and even increases hormones needed for displaying dominance. This in turn helps us become better negotiators and abstract thinkers.\nWhile a good suit works wonders for our performance in the boardroom, wearing formal wear isn't a great idea when we want to socialize. Studies have found that people tend to be less open and less able to relax when they wear formal clothes.\nOn the other hand, a casual dress helps us become more friendly and creative. These findings support the idea of wearing business casuals on a Friday; since colleagues are most likely to take out time to socialize on the last work day of the week. I mean, who wants to hang out with people in their suits?", "47": "We all take a little extra effort to look nice for special occasions. But most of us have conflicting feelings about dressing up and feel guilty about taking the time to focus on clothes. Science now suggests the right dress may give ourselves the extra edge in our professional and personal lives.\nWe hear sayings like \"dress for the job you want; not the job you have\". Most people don't really believe in them, but research into the impact of clothes on behavior now suggests that there may actually be a grain of truth in these sayings. Science says that the clothes we wear affect our behavior, our mood and even the way we interact with others because of the symbolic meaning that we assign to different types of clothing.\nWe consider some clothes to be powerful, some to be fun, and so on. We even evaluate people whom we have just met based on their clothes. We also evaluate ourselves based on what we are wearing because of the way they make us feel. This means that the experience of wearing something affects our attitudes and our choice of behavior.\nThere's a reason tailored jackets are associated with being 'dressed for success'. It seems that wearing formal office wear puts us in the right frame of mind to conduct business. Wearing power clothing makes us feel more confident and even increases hormones needed for displaying dominance. This in turn helps us become better negotiators and abstract thinkers.\nWhile a good suit works wonders for our performance in the boardroom, wearing formal wear isn't a great idea when we want to socialize. Studies have found that people tend to be less open and less able to relax when they wear formal clothes.\nOn the other hand, a casual dress helps us become more friendly and creative. These findings support the idea of wearing business casuals on a Friday; since colleagues are most likely to take out time to socialize on the last work day of the week. I mean, who wants to hang out with people in their suits?", "48": "We all take a little extra effort to look nice for special occasions. But most of us have conflicting feelings about dressing up and feel guilty about taking the time to focus on clothes. Science now suggests the right dress may give ourselves the extra edge in our professional and personal lives.\nWe hear sayings like \"dress for the job you want; not the job you have\". Most people don't really believe in them, but research into the impact of clothes on behavior now suggests that there may actually be a grain of truth in these sayings. Science says that the clothes we wear affect our behavior, our mood and even the way we interact with others because of the symbolic meaning that we assign to different types of clothing.\nWe consider some clothes to be powerful, some to be fun, and so on. We even evaluate people whom we have just met based on their clothes. We also evaluate ourselves based on what we are wearing because of the way they make us feel. This means that the experience of wearing something affects our attitudes and our choice of behavior.\nThere's a reason tailored jackets are associated with being 'dressed for success'. It seems that wearing formal office wear puts us in the right frame of mind to conduct business. Wearing power clothing makes us feel more confident and even increases hormones needed for displaying dominance. This in turn helps us become better negotiators and abstract thinkers.\nWhile a good suit works wonders for our performance in the boardroom, wearing formal wear isn't a great idea when we want to socialize. Studies have found that people tend to be less open and less able to relax when they wear formal clothes.\nOn the other hand, a casual dress helps us become more friendly and creative. These findings support the idea of wearing business casuals on a Friday; since colleagues are most likely to take out time to socialize on the last work day of the week. I mean, who wants to hang out with people in their suits?", "49": "We all take a little extra effort to look nice for special occasions. But most of us have conflicting feelings about dressing up and feel guilty about taking the time to focus on clothes. Science now suggests the right dress may give ourselves the extra edge in our professional and personal lives.\nWe hear sayings like \"dress for the job you want; not the job you have\". Most people don't really believe in them, but research into the impact of clothes on behavior now suggests that there may actually be a grain of truth in these sayings. Science says that the clothes we wear affect our behavior, our mood and even the way we interact with others because of the symbolic meaning that we assign to different types of clothing.\nWe consider some clothes to be powerful, some to be fun, and so on. We even evaluate people whom we have just met based on their clothes. We also evaluate ourselves based on what we are wearing because of the way they make us feel. This means that the experience of wearing something affects our attitudes and our choice of behavior.\nThere's a reason tailored jackets are associated with being 'dressed for success'. It seems that wearing formal office wear puts us in the right frame of mind to conduct business. Wearing power clothing makes us feel more confident and even increases hormones needed for displaying dominance. This in turn helps us become better negotiators and abstract thinkers.\nWhile a good suit works wonders for our performance in the boardroom, wearing formal wear isn't a great idea when we want to socialize. Studies have found that people tend to be less open and less able to relax when they wear formal clothes.\nOn the other hand, a casual dress helps us become more friendly and creative. These findings support the idea of wearing business casuals on a Friday; since colleagues are most likely to take out time to socialize on the last work day of the week. I mean, who wants to hang out with people in their suits?", "50": "We all take a little extra effort to look nice for special occasions. But most of us have conflicting feelings about dressing up and feel guilty about taking the time to focus on clothes. Science now suggests the right dress may give ourselves the extra edge in our professional and personal lives.\nWe hear sayings like \"dress for the job you want; not the job you have\". Most people don't really believe in them, but research into the impact of clothes on behavior now suggests that there may actually be a grain of truth in these sayings. Science says that the clothes we wear affect our behavior, our mood and even the way we interact with others because of the symbolic meaning that we assign to different types of clothing.\nWe consider some clothes to be powerful, some to be fun, and so on. We even evaluate people whom we have just met based on their clothes. We also evaluate ourselves based on what we are wearing because of the way they make us feel. This means that the experience of wearing something affects our attitudes and our choice of behavior.\nThere's a reason tailored jackets are associated with being 'dressed for success'. It seems that wearing formal office wear puts us in the right frame of mind to conduct business. Wearing power clothing makes us feel more confident and even increases hormones needed for displaying dominance. This in turn helps us become better negotiators and abstract thinkers.\nWhile a good suit works wonders for our performance in the boardroom, wearing formal wear isn't a great idea when we want to socialize. Studies have found that people tend to be less open and less able to relax when they wear formal clothes.\nOn the other hand, a casual dress helps us become more friendly and creative. These findings support the idea of wearing business casuals on a Friday; since colleagues are most likely to take out time to socialize on the last work day of the week. I mean, who wants to hang out with people in their suits?", "51": "With the rise of pop music, jazz, and electronic music, both opera and classical music started to fade away from the public eye. Some people are beginning to wonder whether opera and classical music are still relevant to the modern world of music. Granted, you will not typically see today's teenagers lending their ears to Bach anytime soon, but there are some major indicators that both opera and classical music are now still quite alive.\nThe most major indicator of classical music's importance in society today is the fact that much of the popular music that is currently being produced uses similar beats, harmonies, and melodies(旋律) as those that were used in some of classical music's best works. Even so, it can be difficult for those who do not study music theory to see this as an indicator, since it is subtle and just shows the impact symphonic(交响乐的) orchestras have had on society's taste in music.\nA better example for the relevance of opera and classical music can be seen in the invention of the rock opera. Opera, in its simplest definition, is telling a story using music as its form. The art of telling a story using music has not faded in the least bit. In fact, sometimes actual orchestras are used for major parts of the opera itself. Some of the world's greatest hits have been parts of rock operas.\nFans of classical music can also tell you that there are few types of music that are more expressive. So, it should come as no surprise to anyone that classical music pieces are still used as background music in modern movies. Symphonic orchestra compositions have also been created solely for the purpose of being included in major motion pictures. These are often very well received amongst mainstream music fans.\nClassical music and opera are the very foundation of what our modern music is based upon. Considering the huge impact they have had on our current society, it is without doubt that we can expect them to continue to remain important for centuries to come.", "52": "With the rise of pop music, jazz, and electronic music, both opera and classical music started to fade away from the public eye. Some people are beginning to wonder whether opera and classical music are still relevant to the modern world of music. Granted, you will not typically see today's teenagers lending their ears to Bach anytime soon, but there are some major indicators that both opera and classical music are now still quite alive.\nThe most major indicator of classical music's importance in society today is the fact that much of the popular music that is currently being produced uses similar beats, harmonies, and melodies(旋律) as those that were used in some of classical music's best works. Even so, it can be difficult for those who do not study music theory to see this as an indicator, since it is subtle and just shows the impact symphonic(交响乐的) orchestras have had on society's taste in music.\nA better example for the relevance of opera and classical music can be seen in the invention of the rock opera. Opera, in its simplest definition, is telling a story using music as its form. The art of telling a story using music has not faded in the least bit. In fact, sometimes actual orchestras are used for major parts of the opera itself. Some of the world's greatest hits have been parts of rock operas.\nFans of classical music can also tell you that there are few types of music that are more expressive. So, it should come as no surprise to anyone that classical music pieces are still used as background music in modern movies. Symphonic orchestra compositions have also been created solely for the purpose of being included in major motion pictures. These are often very well received amongst mainstream music fans.\nClassical music and opera are the very foundation of what our modern music is based upon. Considering the huge impact they have had on our current society, it is without doubt that we can expect them to continue to remain important for centuries to come.", "53": "With the rise of pop music, jazz, and electronic music, both opera and classical music started to fade away from the public eye. Some people are beginning to wonder whether opera and classical music are still relevant to the modern world of music. Granted, you will not typically see today's teenagers lending their ears to Bach anytime soon, but there are some major indicators that both opera and classical music are now still quite alive.\nThe most major indicator of classical music's importance in society today is the fact that much of the popular music that is currently being produced uses similar beats, harmonies, and melodies(旋律) as those that were used in some of classical music's best works. Even so, it can be difficult for those who do not study music theory to see this as an indicator, since it is subtle and just shows the impact symphonic(交响乐的) orchestras have had on society's taste in music.\nA better example for the relevance of opera and classical music can be seen in the invention of the rock opera. Opera, in its simplest definition, is telling a story using music as its form. The art of telling a story using music has not faded in the least bit. In fact, sometimes actual orchestras are used for major parts of the opera itself. Some of the world's greatest hits have been parts of rock operas.\nFans of classical music can also tell you that there are few types of music that are more expressive. So, it should come as no surprise to anyone that classical music pieces are still used as background music in modern movies. Symphonic orchestra compositions have also been created solely for the purpose of being included in major motion pictures. These are often very well received amongst mainstream music fans.\nClassical music and opera are the very foundation of what our modern music is based upon. Considering the huge impact they have had on our current society, it is without doubt that we can expect them to continue to remain important for centuries to come.", "54": "With the rise of pop music, jazz, and electronic music, both opera and classical music started to fade away from the public eye. Some people are beginning to wonder whether opera and classical music are still relevant to the modern world of music. Granted, you will not typically see today's teenagers lending their ears to Bach anytime soon, but there are some major indicators that both opera and classical music are now still quite alive.\nThe most major indicator of classical music's importance in society today is the fact that much of the popular music that is currently being produced uses similar beats, harmonies, and melodies(旋律) as those that were used in some of classical music's best works. Even so, it can be difficult for those who do not study music theory to see this as an indicator, since it is subtle and just shows the impact symphonic(交响乐的) orchestras have had on society's taste in music.\nA better example for the relevance of opera and classical music can be seen in the invention of the rock opera. Opera, in its simplest definition, is telling a story using music as its form. The art of telling a story using music has not faded in the least bit. In fact, sometimes actual orchestras are used for major parts of the opera itself. Some of the world's greatest hits have been parts of rock operas.\nFans of classical music can also tell you that there are few types of music that are more expressive. So, it should come as no surprise to anyone that classical music pieces are still used as background music in modern movies. Symphonic orchestra compositions have also been created solely for the purpose of being included in major motion pictures. These are often very well received amongst mainstream music fans.\nClassical music and opera are the very foundation of what our modern music is based upon. Considering the huge impact they have had on our current society, it is without doubt that we can expect them to continue to remain important for centuries to come.", "55": "With the rise of pop music, jazz, and electronic music, both opera and classical music started to fade away from the public eye. Some people are beginning to wonder whether opera and classical music are still relevant to the modern world of music. Granted, you will not typically see today's teenagers lending their ears to Bach anytime soon, but there are some major indicators that both opera and classical music are now still quite alive.\nThe most major indicator of classical music's importance in society today is the fact that much of the popular music that is currently being produced uses similar beats, harmonies, and melodies(旋律) as those that were used in some of classical music's best works. Even so, it can be difficult for those who do not study music theory to see this as an indicator, since it is subtle and just shows the impact symphonic(交响乐的) orchestras have had on society's taste in music.\nA better example for the relevance of opera and classical music can be seen in the invention of the rock opera. Opera, in its simplest definition, is telling a story using music as its form. The art of telling a story using music has not faded in the least bit. In fact, sometimes actual orchestras are used for major parts of the opera itself. Some of the world's greatest hits have been parts of rock operas.\nFans of classical music can also tell you that there are few types of music that are more expressive. So, it should come as no surprise to anyone that classical music pieces are still used as background music in modern movies. Symphonic orchestra compositions have also been created solely for the purpose of being included in major motion pictures. These are often very well received amongst mainstream music fans.\nClassical music and opera are the very foundation of what our modern music is based upon. Considering the huge impact they have had on our current society, it is without doubt that we can expect them to continue to remain important for centuries to come."}, "questions": [{"id": "2025-06-2-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What does the news report say about Vaclav Bursa and Joanna Stephens?", "options": ["A) They are young teenagers from Atlanta.", "B) They won this year's 7 Mile Bridge Run.", "C) They completed the race in less than 40 minutes.", "D) They are both students at Marathon High School."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） They won this year's 7 Mile Bridge Run.\n【定位】Vaclav Bursa, 15, of Big Pine Key, Florida, finished first overall. ... Joanna Stephens, 28, of\nAtlanta, completed the course in 41 minutes and 12 seconds. She won the women's division\ntitle and placed second overall.\n来自佛罗里达州大派恩礁的 15 岁的瓦茨拉夫·布尔萨获得总排名第一。……来自亚特兰大的\n28 岁的乔安娜·斯蒂芬斯以 41 分 12 秒完成赛程。她赢得女子组冠军，总排名第二。\n【信号】两人各自的名次陈述。答案句不靠转折词，而靠「人物+成绩」的并列陈述定位：finished\nfirst overall 和 won the women's division title 分别说明两人都拿了各自组别第一——他\n们都是「这场 7 Mile Bridge Run 的获胜者」，这正是选项 B 的概括。\n【替换】finished first overall ↔ won this year's 7 Mile Bridge Run\n「总排名第一」=「赢了这场比赛」\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\n选项盗了原文真实出现的地名「Atlanta」诱你听到就选，但 Atlanta 只属于 28 岁的\nStephens；15 岁的 Bursa 来自 Florida。说两人「都是来自亚特兰大的青少年」既错配了\n城市，又把 28 岁的人也算成 teenager，双重不符。\n借用原文：Joanna Stephens, 28, of Atlanta\nC) 与原文相反\n原文用时是 41 minutes and 1 second 与 41 minutes and 12 seconds，都是 41 分多，\n明明大于 40 分钟；选项偏说「less than 40 minutes（少于 40 分钟）」。它盗用了「分钟\n数」这个耳熟数字，却把实际 41 多分钟篡改成「不到 40 分」，方向正好相反。\n借用原文：41 minutes and 1 second\nD) 原词陷阱\n「Marathon High School」原文确实出现，极易让人听到就选；但原文只说 He is a\nstudent at Marathon High School，这个「He」指 Bursa 一人。选项说两人「都是\n（both）」该校学生，把只属于 Bursa 的身份硬扣给了 Stephens。\n借用原文：He is a student at Marathon High School", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-2-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "What do we learn about the April contest?", "options": ["A) It drew competitors from more than 40 countries.", "B) It is watched live online by fans all over the world.", "C) It closed the city of Big Pine Key to traffic for three hours.", "D) It is one of the most popular running events in the southeastern U.S."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It is one of the most popular running events in the southeastern U.S.\n【定位】The April contest is one of the most popular running events in the southeastern United States.\n这场四月的赛事是美国东南部最受欢迎的跑步活动之一。\n【信号】最高级定性句即结论。答案句靠最高级信号锁定：one of the most popular running\nevents in the southeastern United States 是对赛事地位的定性总结，几乎与选项 D 逐字\n对应。听到「one of the most…」这种最高级评价句，要立刻意识到它往往就是某道题的\n答案落点。\n【替换】one of the most popular running events in the southeastern United States ↔\nIt is one of the most popular running events in the southeastern U.S.\n几乎逐字对应，仅缩写化\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\n原文说 drew competitors from 41 US states——来自 41 个「州」，另有 Canada, the\nUK and Switzerland 等少数几国选手。选项盗用「40 多」这个数量级，却把「states\n（州）」偷换成「countries（国家）」，参赛国其实只有零星几个，远到不了 40 个。\n借用原文：The race drew competitors from 41 US states.\nB) 未提及\n原文涉及「online」的只有 online registration opened in January——指线上「报名」，\n全文从未说有人「在线观看直播（watched live online）」。选项把「线上报名」悄悄换成\n「全球粉丝线上观看」，是没有原文支撑的编造。\n借用原文：after online registration opened in January\nC) 原词陷阱\n原文是 closed the span between the Middle and Lower Keys to traffic for 3 hours\n——封的是中、下礁岛之间的那段「桥/路」三小时；选项把「3 hours」和「closed」原样\n搬来，却说封的是「the city of Big Pine Key（整座大派恩礁城）」，封闭对象被偷换了。\n借用原文：The run closed the span between the Middle and Lower Keys to traffic for 3 hours\nQuestions 3 and 4 are based on the news report you have just heard.\n1 British astronaut Tim Peake is stepping down permanently from his role as a European astronaut. He's\ngoing to take up a full-time position as ambassador for science and space instead—work he's been doing\nsince 2019. The former British Army Air Corps helicopter pilot was selected as a European Space\nAgency astronaut in 2009. He flew to the space station for a 6-month tour in 2015. The tour saw him\ncontrol a robot on earth remotely from orbit. He also helped dock two spacecraft. Tim said, \"Being an\nastronaut has been the most extraordinary experience. I've had the privilege of working with an\nexceptional team of individuals during the past 13 years with the agency. It has been incredibly exciting\nand rewarding. By assuming the role of an ambassador for human flights to space, I'll continue to\nsupport the European and the UK space agencies.\"", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-2-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What do we learn from the news report about Tim Peake?", "options": ["A) He was once a full-time ambassador.", "B) He was soon to retire from the army.", "C) He became an astronaut in 2015.", "D) He used to be a helicopter pilot."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） He used to be a helicopter pilot.\n【定位】The former British Army Air Corps helicopter pilot was selected as a European Space Agency\nastronaut in 2009.\n这位前英国陆军航空兵的直升机飞行员于 2009 年被选为欧洲航天局的宇航员。\n【信号】former 点出'过去身份'。答案句开头的 The former British Army Air Corps helicopter\npilot 里 former(过去的)就是信号词：它直接交代了 Tim Peake 过去的身份是直升机飞行\n员，正对 Q3 '关于他我们了解到什么'。\n【替换】The former British Army Air Corps helicopter pilot ↔ He used to be a helicopter pilot.\nformer 名词 = used to be 动词\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音确实念到 ambassador，但说的是他将去 take up a full-time position as\nambassador——这是接下来要担任的新职，不是过去就当过；A 把'即将担任'说成 was\nonce(曾经是),时间方向错了。\n借用原文：He's going to take up a full-time position as ambassador for science and space\nB) 偷换概念\n录音说的是他从'欧洲宇航员'岗位上 stepping down permanently from his role as a\nEuropean astronaut(永久卸任宇航员角色)，并非从军队退役；B 把'卸任宇航员'偷换成'即\n将从军队退役'，army 一词也只在他过去身份里出现，并未说他要退役。\n借用原文：Tim Peake is stepping down permanently from his role as a European astronaut\nC) 张冠李戴\n录音里 2015 对应的是 He flew to the space station for a 6-month tour in 2015.(2015\n年飞往空间站)，而'被选为宇航员'是 selected as a European Space Agency astronaut in\n2009(2009 年)；C 把 astronaut 与 2015 错配，年份张冠李戴。\n借用原文：He flew to the space station for a 6-month tour in 2015.", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-2-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What does Tim Peake say about his work with the European Space Agency?", "options": ["A) It has been unbelievably exciting and rewarding.", "B) It has been extraordinarily tough and demanding.", "C) It has contributed a great deal to human flights to space.", "D) It has given him exceptional privilege as an ambassador."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （态度观点题） It has been unbelievably exciting and rewarding.\n【定位】It has been incredibly exciting and rewarding.\n这(段经历)一直非常激动人心、收获满满。\n【信号】引语里的评价形容词即落点。态度题的落点在当事人自述的评价词。录音引语里 It has\nbeen incredibly exciting and rewarding. 用 exciting、rewarding 两个褒义形容词直接给\n出他对工作的评价，正是答案句。\n【替换】incredibly exciting and rewarding ↔ unbelievably exciting and rewarding\nincredibly = unbelievably 同义替换\n【排除】B) 与原文相反\nextraordinarily tough and demanding 是负面评价，但他原话是 incredibly exciting and\nrewarding(激动、有收获)这类褒义；B 把好评偷换成差评，极性正相反。录音里确有\nextraordinary 一词(说 the most extraordinary experience)，B 借这个原词制造迷惑。\n借用原文：Being an astronaut has been the most extraordinary experience.\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音确有 an ambassador for human flights to space 这串词，但那是讲他将担任的新角\n色，不是评价这份工作'对航天贡献巨大'；题干问他怎么评价工作，C 答成'贡献'，是听到原\n词就选的答非所问。\n借用原文：By assuming the role of an ambassador for human flights to space\nD) 张冠李戴\n录音里 privilege 出现在 I've had the privilege of working with an exceptional team(有\n幸与优秀团队共事)，讲的是与团队合作的荣幸，不是'作为 ambassador 的特殊荣誉'；D 把\nprivilege 和 ambassador 硬凑到一起，张冠李戴。\n借用原文：I've had the privilege of working with an exceptional team of individuals", "points": ["听力·长对话", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-2-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What do we learn about the five cats seen in the Asda store?", "options": ["A) They are known for their loyalty to their owners.", "B) They are brought in by the store's customers.", "C) They are regular visitors to the store.", "D) They are pets kept by the store staff."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） They are regular visitors to the store.\n【定位】Regular shoppers at the supermarket are familiar with the sight of several cats settling down\nin the shop aisles. There are now at least five different cats who come almost on a daily basis.\n超市的常客们对几只猫在店内过道里安顿下来的景象已经习以为常。如今至少有五只不同的\n猫几乎天天都来。\n【信号】客观陈述句直接交代猫的常态。新闻里没有转折，答案就藏在平铺直叙交代事实的句子里：\nseveral cats settling down in the shop aisles 配上 five different cats who come\nalmost on a daily basis，直接说明这几只猫是几乎天天来店里的常客。听到 \"come\nalmost on a daily basis\"（几乎每天都来）就该锁定这是答案句。\n【替换】five different cats who come almost on a daily basis ↔ regular visitors to the store\n\"几乎每天来\"=\"常客\"\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nloyalty to their owners 抓住了录音里的 loyal 这个原词，可录音 The supermarket is\nknown for its loyal customers 说的是超市以\"忠实顾客\"闻名，忠诚指的是人、不是猫，更\n没说猫忠于主人。听到 loyal 就选 A，正中原词陷阱。\n借用原文：The supermarket is known for its loyal customers\nB) 未提及\nbrought in by the store's customers 听起来合理，但录音明说猫是 wandered in and\nmade themselves comfortable（自己晃进来安顿下来），第一只是 one cat routinely\nstarted visiting the store，没有任何\"顾客带进来\"的内容，属凭常识硬安、原文未提及。\n借用原文：wandered in and made themselves comfortable\nD) 原词陷阱\npets kept by the store staff 借了录音里的 staff 和 pet 两个原词，可录音里 Staff did\ntheir best to discourage the pet from hanging around 说的是店员想方设法\"赶\"猫、不\n让它逗留，恰恰说明猫不归店员所有。听到 staff、pet 就选 D，又是原词陷阱。\n借用原文：Staff did their best to discourage the pet from hanging around", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-2-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "What did some customers think when they saw the first cat routinely appear in the store?", "options": ["A) It might be a nuisance to the loyal shoppers.", "B) It might have been abandoned by its owner.", "C) It might be 12 months old.", "D) It might have got lost."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It might have got lost.\n【定位】Local newspapers reported that puzzled customers posted on Facebook groups to try to find\nout if the cat was lost.\n当地报纸报道说，困惑的顾客在脸书群组里发帖，想弄清楚这只猫是不是走丢了。\n【信号】顾客行为句直接道出\"以为它丢了\"。描述顾客当时反应的句子就是答案句：puzzled\ncustomers posted on Facebook groups to try to find out if the cat was lost——困惑\n的顾客发帖打听\"猫是不是走丢了\"。其中 if the cat was lost 直接点出顾客脑中的念头。听\n到 puzzled（困惑的）和 lost 就该锁定。\n【替换】to try to find out if the cat was lost ↔ It might have got lost.\n\"想弄清是否走丢\"=\"它可能走丢了\"\n【排除】A) 答非所问\nnuisance to the loyal shoppers 借了 loyal/shoppers 的影子，但录音里顾客看到第一只\n猫时是 puzzled（困惑），去打听它是否走丢，并没有觉得它\"惹人嫌\"。猫多到要贴告示是\n后文的事，且也没说它惹忠实顾客嫌，时间和内容都对不上。\n借用原文：puzzled customers\nB) 偷换概念\nabandoned by its owner 与正解贴得最近，都是\"它出事了\"，但录音里顾客打听的是 if\nthe cat was lost（是否走丢），是\"丢\"不是\"主人不要了\"。况且下文 its owner assured\nthe concerned customers it was very much loved 说主人很爱它，根本没被遗弃。错在\n偷换了\"走丢\"与\"被弃\"这两个相近却不同的概念。\n借用原文：if the cat was lost\nC) 原词陷阱\nIt might be 12 months old. 抓住录音真念过的 12 个月，但录音 The unusual situation\nstarted about 12 months ago 说的是\"这桩怪事大约始于 12 个月前\"，12 个月是\"猫开始\n来店多久\"，根本不是\"猫 12 个月大\"。听到 12 months 就选 C，是典型的数字原词陷阱。\n借用原文：The unusual situation started about 12 months ago", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-2-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "What are customers asked not to do?", "options": ["A) Feed the cats.", "B) Remove any store signs.", "C) Sit in the shop aisles.", "D) Bring in pets with them."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Feed the cats.\n【定位】They visit the store so much that staff have been forced to put up signs, asking their\ncustomers not to feed them.\n这些猫太常来店里，以至于店员不得不张贴告示，要求顾客不要喂它们。\n【信号】请求句 asking…not to 直接给出禁止内容。答案句末尾的请求结构就是定位信号：asking\ntheir customers not to feed them（要求顾客不要喂它们）。听到 not to + 动词，紧跟\n的 feed 就是被禁止的那件事。这是\"听要求句、抓 not to 后面的动作\"的标准落点。\n【替换】not to feed them ↔ Feed the cats.\n\"不要喂它们\"对应被禁动作\"喂猫\"\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nRemove any store signs. 抓住录音真念过的 signs，可录音 staff have been forced to\nput up signs 说的是店员\"贴\"告示，从没要求顾客\"撤\"告示。听到 signs 就选 B，正中原词\n陷阱，且把\"贴\"反成了\"撤\"。\n借用原文：staff have been forced to put up signs\nC) 张冠李戴\nSit in the shop aisles. 借了录音原词 aisles，可录音 several cats settling down in the\nshop aisles 说的是猫在过道里安顿，不是顾客坐过道，更没人要求顾客别坐。听到 aisles\n就选 C，是原词陷阱加张冠李戴。\n借用原文：several cats settling down in the shop aisles\nD) 未提及\nBring in pets with them. 蹭了录音里的 pet 一词，但录音里店员唯一对顾客提的要求是\nnot to feed them（别喂猫），从没说过\"别带宠物进店\"。带宠物进店是凭常识联想出来\n的，原文并未提及。\n借用原文：asking their customers not to feed them", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-2-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "What has the woman been thinking of doing next semester?", "options": ["A) Sharing her room with friends.", "B) Cutting down her living expenses.", "C) Living off campus.", "D) Moving to the city center."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Living off campus.\n【定位】W: I've been thinking about moving off campus next semester.\n女：我一直在考虑下学期搬到校外去住。\n【信号】开场首句直陈打算。信号是女方对话开场第一句的直接陈述 I've been thinking about\nmoving off campus next semester.，与题干 thinking of doing 完全对应——这就是承\n载答案的那句。\n【替换】moving off campus ↔ Living off campus\nmoving=living 同指搬离校园住\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\nSharing her room with friends. 借了后文女方说的 Three of my friends from the\ndormitory would share the place with me，但那是『四人合租整套房子』，不是『与朋\n友合住一个房间』，且非她最初的打算，是把后文细节错安到本题。\n借用原文：Three of my friends from the dormitory would share the place with me\nB) 原词陷阱\nCutting down her living expenses. 盗用了原文反复出现的 the expense 一词，但女方的\n打算是搬出校外，省钱只是搬出后讨论的一个方面，不是她『在考虑做的事』本身，听到\nexpense 就选会落坑。\n借用原文：And then there's the expense, too.\nD) 与原文相反\nMoving to the city center. 与原文正相反：女方说的是 if we move far enough from the\ncity center（搬到离市中心足够远的地方），选项却说搬向市中心，极性反了。\n借用原文：if we move far enough from the city center", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-2-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What does the man suggest the woman take into consideration?", "options": ["A) The facilities available.", "B) Various costs involved.", "C) Changes in living conditions.", "D) The distance to her home."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Various costs involved.\n【定位】M: Are you sure about that? Rents in big cities are high, and then there's the cost of electricity\nand gas. Have you factored those in?\n男：你确定吗？大城市的房租很高，还有电费和燃气费。这些你都算进去了吗？\n【信号】连列费用项+反问提醒。信号是男方一口气列出 Rents in big cities are high, and then\nthere's the cost of electricity and gas 并反问 Have you factored those in?——他反复\n强调、要女方算进去的正是各项费用，这就是答案句。\n【替换】Rents in big cities are high, and then there's the cost of electricity and gas ↔\nVarious costs involved\n罗列各项费用=概括为各种费用\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\nThe facilities available. 看似贴居住话题，但原文男方提醒的是费用，厨房、卧室等设施是\n后文女方自己列搬出去的好处 We'd have a kitchen，并非男方让她『考虑』的事，张冠李\n戴。\n借用原文：We'd have a kitchen, so we could cook ourselves.\nC) 偷换概念\nChanges in living conditions. 借用了贯穿全程的 living 一词，但男方让考虑的是『费用』\n这一具体方面，不是笼统的『生活条件变化』，把具体范畴偷换成宽泛说法。\n借用原文：if we move far enough from the city center, the rent is low enough\nD) 原词陷阱\nThe distance to her home. 盗用了男方开头 so far from home 里的 home 一词，但那是\n男方表达担忧的话，并非他要女方『纳入考虑』的方面，且真正反复提醒的是费用，听到\nhome 就选会落坑。\n借用原文：living all by yourself in an apartment so far from home", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-2-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "What does the woman say she did during high school?", "options": ["A) Went through much trouble.", "B) Commuted to save money.", "C) Studied on the bus.", "D) Lived on campus."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Studied on the bus.\n【定位】W: It's about an hour to campus, but there's no reason I can't study on the bus. After all, I did\nthat during high school and my commute was even longer then.\n女：到学校大约一小时，但我没理由不能在公交车上学习啊。毕竟我高中时就是这么做的，\n那时候我通勤路上的时间还更长呢。\n【信号】时间词 during high school 处的回指。信号是题干时间词在原文的复现 I did that during\nhigh school，而 that 回指紧挨着的 study on the bus——这句就是答案句。\n【替换】I did that during high school ↔ Studied on the bus\nthat 回指 study on the bus\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\nWent through much trouble. 借了男方的话 go through all that trouble，但那是男方对\n『搬出去折腾』的评价，不是女方说她高中做过的事，把男方的话安到女方头上。\n借用原文：go through all that trouble\nB) 原词陷阱\nCommuted to save money. 把原文出现的 commute（my commute was even longer）\n和前文省钱话题拼在一起，但女方说高中时做的是在车上学习，并非『为省钱而通勤』，且\n省钱是合租话题、不是高中经历。\n借用原文：my commute was even longer then\nD) 与原文相反\nLived on campus. 借用了贯穿全程的 campus、live 等词，但女方高中时是通勤\n（commute 路上更长），恰恰说明她当时没住校，选项与原意相反。\n借用原文：my commute was even longer then", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-2-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "What does the man say he'll do at the end of the conversation?", "options": ["A) Discuss the matter with the woman's mother.", "B) Make a decision within a few days.", "C) Try his best to persuade his wife.", "D) Weigh the benefits against the disadvantages."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Discuss the matter with the woman's mother.\n【定位】M: I'm not convinced that the benefits are greater than the disadvantages, but I'll discuss this\nwith your mother.\n男：我还不太相信好处大过坏处，不过我会和你妈妈商量一下这件事。\n【信号】转折 but 后的 I'll 表态。信号是结尾男方转折词 but 之后的 I'll discuss this with your\nmother——but 之后才是他真正要做的事，这就是答案句。\n【替换】I'll discuss this with your mother ↔ Discuss the matter with the woman's mother\nthis=the matter、your mother=woman's mother\n【排除】B) 未提及\nMake a decision within a few days. 里 within a few days 录音从头到尾没出现，是凭\n『商量后该尽快决定』的常识脑补出来的时限，原文未提。\nC) 偷换概念\nTry his best to persuade his wife. 把男方要找 mother（即他妻子）这层关系拿来，却说\n成『努力说服妻子』，可原文是 discuss this with your mother——是平等商量，不是单\n方说服，把『商量』偷换成『说服』。\n借用原文：I'll discuss this with your mother\nD) 原词陷阱\nWeigh the benefits against the disadvantages. 盗用了同句 the benefits are greater\nthan the disadvantages 的原词，但那是男方表达『还没被说服』的态度（but 之前），不\n是他说接下来要做的事；他要做的是商量，听到 benefits/disadvantages 就选会落坑。\n借用原文：I'm not convinced that the benefits are greater than the disadvantages", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-2-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "What is the woman doing?", "options": ["A) Interviewing the man.", "B) Looking for Lawrence.", "C) Choosing a present for her nephew.", "D) Helping the man with his shopping."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Choosing a present for her nephew.\n【定位】W: Good morning. I'm looking for a present for my nephew, Lawrence.\n女：早上好。我在给我侄子劳伦斯找一份礼物。\n【信号】开场自报来意。女士一开口就用 I'm looking for a present for my nephew 交代了来店目\n的，这种\"开场自述要做什么\"的句子就是 What doing 题的答案句。\n【替换】looking for a present for my nephew ↔ Choosing a present for her nephew\nlooking for=Choosing 同义改写\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\n全程是女士向店员咨询、店员介绍商品，没有任何采访行为；Interviewing the man. 把\"咨\n询\"误当\"采访\"，且弄反了谁主导对话。\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音确实念到了 Lawrence，但 Lawrence 是要送礼的侄子，女士并不是在\"找劳伦斯本\n人\"；Looking for Lawrence. 偷了这个人名来骗\"听到就选\"。\n借用原文：I'm looking for a present for my nephew, Lawrence\nD) 与原文相反\n是店员（男士）在帮女士选礼物，不是女士帮男士购物；Helping the man with his\nshopping. 把帮忙的方向反了过来。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-2-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "How does the man describe Bobo Man, the action figure?", "options": ["A) Quite educational.", "B) Incredibly popular.", "C) Driven by batteries.", "D) Obviously childish."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Incredibly popular.\n【定位】M: For that age group, our biggest seller this year has been this action figure. It's called Bobo\nMan, and it's incredibly popular.\n男：对这个年龄段，我们今年卖得最好的就是这个人偶。它叫 Bobo Man，非常受欢迎。\n【信号】店员正面推介的形容。男士介绍这件玩具时直接说 it's incredibly popular，这句正面评价\n就是\"他如何描述它\"的答案句。\n【替换】it's incredibly popular ↔ Incredibly popular.\n原句直取，几乎零改写\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\neducational 确实在录音出现，但那是女士后来问 something educational、指向拼图，并\n非男士对 Bobo Man 的描述；Quite educational. 把后文别处的词搬来安到 Bobo Man 头\n上。\n借用原文：Perhaps something educational\nC) 与原文相反\nbatteries 在录音里出现，但男士明确说 It has no batteries——它\"没有\"电池；Driven by\nbatteries. 取了原词却反了意思。\n借用原文：It has no batteries\nD) 未提及\n录音从未把 Bobo Man 说成幼稚；男士只说它受欢迎、是动画主角。Obviously childish.\n属于凭印象编的负面评价，原文无据。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-2-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "What kind of game does the woman think would be good for Lawrence?", "options": ["A) One he loves for helping exercise his mind.", "B) One he can play together with his sister.", "C) One he finds interesting and challenging.", "D) One he can play either alone or in a group."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） One he can play together with his sister.\n【定位】W: I like this sort of idea. Lawrence has a sister who is slightly older, so maybe a game that\nthey can play together would be good.\n女：我喜欢这个主意。劳伦斯有个稍大一点的姐姐，所以也许一款他们能一起玩的游戏会\n不错。\n【信号】女士本人 so...would be good 的结论。女士先说有姐姐，再用 so maybe a game that\nthey can play together would be good 给出她看重的条件——\"两人一起玩\"，这正是\nthink...good 的答案句。\n【替换】a game that they can play together ↔ One he can play together with his sister\nthey=他和姐姐，指代还原\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\nexercising the mind 出现在录音，但那是男士介绍拼图时说 great for exercising the\nmind，不是女士看重的理由；题干问的是女士的想法，故 One he loves for helping\nexercise his mind. 张冠李戴。\n借用原文：great for exercising the mind\nC) 未提及\n女士没有说看重\"有趣又有挑战\"；challenging 一词录音根本没出现。One he finds\ninteresting and challenging. 是凭印象编的卖点。\nD) 张冠李戴\nplay either alone or in groups 确在录音，但那是男士介绍拼图的卖点 can be played\neither alone or in groups，不是女士对劳伦斯看重的点；女士看重的是\"和姐姐一起\"。\nOne he can play either alone or in a group. 把店员的话当成女士的判断。\n借用原文：can be played either alone or in groups", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-2-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What do we learn from the conversation about the game Battleships?", "options": ["A) It has been enjoyed by kids for decades.", "B) It always reminds people of times of war.", "C) It has been played both indoors and outdoors.", "D) It distinguishes itself as one of the oldest games."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It has been enjoyed by kids for decades.\n【定位】M: Of course you did. Everyone did. And kids enjoy it just as much today as you and I did\nwhen we were young. Some games just never get old.\n男：你当然玩过。人人都玩过。而且如今的孩子玩它玩得和当年的你我一样开心。有些游戏\n就是永不过时。\n【信号】多年仍受孩子喜爱的信息点。男士说 kids enjoy it just as much today as you and I did\nwhen we were young，加上前文 It has been around since the 1930s，合起来支持\"几\n十年来一直被孩子喜爱\"。\n【替换】It has been around since the 1930s ↔ It has been enjoyed by kids for decades\nsince the 1930s=for decades\nkids enjoy it just as much today as you and I did when we were young ↔\nIt has been enjoyed by kids for decades\n孩子持续喜爱=enjoyed by kids\n【排除】B) 过度推断\n录音只说这游戏经典、不过时，从没说它\"让人想起战争\"。It always reminds people of\ntimes of war. 是看 Battleships（战舰）这名字凭空联想，原文无据，且 always 太绝对。\nC) 原词陷阱\n女士提到 we had to stay indoors（下雨只能待室内玩），录音只说室内，从没说室外也\n玩；It has been played both indoors and outdoors. 偷了 indoors 一词，却擅自加上\noutdoors。\n借用原文：we had to stay indoors\nD) 过度推断\n男士只说它是 a total classic、自 1930 年代就有，并没说它\"是最古老的游戏之一\"；It\ndistinguishes itself as one of the oldest games. 把\"经典\"拔高成\"最古老之一\"，程度过\n头。\n借用原文：It's a total classic", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-2-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "What do we learn from the passage about the paradox of choice?", "options": ["A) A display of a variety of products increases consumers' interest.", "B) Too many choices decrease one's desire to buy.", "C) Fewer choices lead to lower customer satisfaction.", "D) Customers given more choices are more likely to get confused."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Too many choices decrease one's desire to buy.\n【定位】But here's the paradox of choice: if a person is presented with too many choices, they are\nactually less likely to buy.\n但这就是“选择悖论”：如果一个人面对太多选择，他实际上反而更不可能购买。\n【信号】转折 But 加冒号点题，后半才是真意。前一句 We assume that if we offer our\ncustomers more choices, then they'll be more likely to buy 是“我们以为”的错误假设，\n紧跟 But here's the paradox of choice 用转折加冒号正式抛出概念，转折后的内容才是答\n案句。\n【替换】too many choices ↔ Too many choices\n原词同形保留\nless likely to buy ↔ decrease one's desire to buy\n“更不可能买”=“降低购买欲”，同义改写\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 说陈列多样“增加兴趣”，借的是后文 created more interest里的 interest，但本题问的是\nparadox of choice 的核心结论，原文用 But 推翻的正是“选项多就更想买”这种正向假设，\nA 站在被推翻的一边。\n借用原文：created more interest\nC) 与原文相反\nC 说“更少选择导致更低满意度”，盗用了原文 customer satisfaction 一词，但原文 the\nbigger display of jams leads to lower customer satisfaction than the smaller display\n说的是“更多”选择才满意度更低，C 把多少接反了，属与原文相反。\n借用原文：lower customer satisfaction\nD) 未提及\nD 说顾客“get confused”（困惑），听上去像“选择多”的合理后果，但原文从头到尾只说更\n不可能买、满意度更低，从未提到“困惑”，属凭常识脑补的未提及项。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-2-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "What did the psychologists want to find out in their 2012 study about jams?", "options": ["A) What kind of display results in more sales.", "B) What kind of display creates more interest.", "C) What kind of display attracts a greater crowd of customers.", "D) What kind of display leads to greater customer satisfaction."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （目的题） What kind of display results in more sales.\n【定位】Guess which display table led to more sales?\n猜猜哪张陈列桌带来了更多销量？\n【信号】设问句直接点出研究要回答的问题。原文以反问 Guess which display table led to more\nsales?把整段研究的核心问题挑明——研究就是要看“哪种展示带来更多销量”，这个设问句\n即研究目的的答案句。\n【替换】which display table led to more sales ↔ What kind of display results in more sales.\n“哪张桌带来更多销量”=“哪种陈列带来更多销量”\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nB 说研究想找“哪种展示引起更多兴趣”，借了原文 created more interest里的 interest，但\n“更多兴趣”是研究顺带得出的结果，研究真正要找的是销量；B 把结果当成了研究目的。\n借用原文：created more interest\nC) 未提及\nC 说研究想找哪种展示“吸引更大客流”，crowd of customers 原文压根没出现，是凭“大展\n台显眼”的常识脑补出来的，属未提及。\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 说研究想找哪种展示带来“更高满意度”，借了原文 customer satisfaction 一词，但满意\n度同样是研究附带得出的结论，不是 2012 研究设问要找的目标；与 B 同病，混淆结果与目\n的。\n借用原文：lower customer satisfaction", "points": ["听力·讲座", "目的题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-2-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "What is the finding of an extensive study published in 2015?", "options": ["A) There are situations where customers buy nothing.", "B) There are tricks to increase customer satisfaction.", "C) There are ways to predict customer preferences.", "D) There are rules that motivate customers to buy."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） There are rules that motivate customers to buy.\n【定位】They found four rules that motivate consumers to buy: when people want to make a quick and\neasy choice, when the product is complex, when it's difficult to compare alternatives, and\nwhen consumers don't have clear preferences.\n他们发现了促使消费者购买的四条规则：当人们想做一个又快又省事的选择时、当产品很复\n杂时、当难以比较各个备选项时、以及当消费者没有明确偏好时。\n【信号】found 加冒号列举，结论句标志。原文 They found four rules that motivate consumers\nto buy以 found 引出、冒号后接四条列举，是这项 2015 研究的总结论句，正是“finding”\n所在。\n【替换】four rules that motivate consumers to buy ↔ There are rules that motivate customers to buy.\n“motivate consumers to buy”=“motivate customers to buy”，近乎同形\n【排除】A) 未提及\nA 说研究发现“存在顾客什么都不买的情形”，听似贴合“选择多就不买”的主题，但 2015 研\n究的发现明确是“four rules”，原文没说“buy nothing”，属把前文主题硬套到本题、未提\n及。\nB) 张冠李戴\nB 说研究发现“提升满意度的技巧”，借了前文 customer satisfaction 一词，但满意度是前\n一项果酱研究的内容，2015 研究找的是“促买规则”不是“提升满意度的技巧”，张冠李戴且偷\n换概念。\n借用原文：lower customer satisfaction\nC) 偷换概念\nC 说研究发现“预测顾客偏好的方法”，盗用了原文 consumers don't have clear\npreferences里的 preferences，但原文说的是“消费者没有明确偏好”是促买的条件之一，绝\n非“能预测偏好”，属偷换概念。\n借用原文：consumers don't have clear preferences", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-2-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What do many Americans think of volunteering?", "options": ["A) Stressful.", "B) Burdensome.", "C) Worthwhile.", "D) Energy-boosting."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （态度观点题） Worthwhile.\n【定位】According to one survey, only 26% of Americans volunteered, despite reports that many\nAmericans view volunteering as worthwhile.\n据一项调查，只有 26% 的美国人做过志愿服务，尽管有报道称许多美国人认为志愿服务是\n值得做的。\n【信号】题干同词 many Americans 在 despite 让步从句里现身。答案句里 many Americans\nview volunteering as worthwhile 与题干 many Americans 完全对上，且 despite（尽\n管）这个让步信号后面跟的正是‘大家的看法’，听到 despite + many Americans 就该锁定\n这句。\n【替换】view volunteering as worthwhile ↔ Worthwhile.\n原句形容词被直接抽出做选项\n【排除】A) 未提及\n很多人想当然觉得志愿‘让人有压力’，但录音对态度只给了 worthwhile 一个正面评价，从\n没说 stressful，属于用常识代替文本的硬伤。\nB) 与原文相反\n录音明确是 worthwhile（值得、正面），Burdensome（负担重的）是负面，方向相反；\n预读时已把它和 worthwhile 分到对立两组，听出正面语气即可排除。\nD) 答非所问\n录音后文确实讲志愿能 boost happiness、提升健康，本项把这些好处浓缩成‘提神’来诱\n选，但题干问的是 many Americans think of it 的评价，答案句只给了 worthwhile，本项\n答非所问。\n借用原文：boost happiness", "points": ["听力·讲座", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-2-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "How could companies ensure both employees and employers benefit from volunteering?", "options": ["A) By linking it with employees' salary.", "B) By taking it as the New Year resolution.", "C) By improving work schedules.", "D) By making it part of the job."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） By making it part of the job.\n【定位】Most people cite time and conflicting work schedules as major barriers, but employees and\nemployers would actually benefit if companies made employee volunteering part of the job.\n大多数人把时间和工作排期冲突列为主要障碍，但如果公司把员工志愿服务变成工作的一部\n分，员工和雇主其实都会受益。\n【信号】转折 but 之后才是真正答案，且 benefit 与题干呼应。but employees and employers\nwould actually benefit if companies made employee volunteering part of the job\n里，转折词 but 后面才是作者的真意，且 benefit、employees and employers 与题干\nboth…benefit 直接对上，听到 but 紧跟 benefit 就锁定这句。\n【替换】made employee volunteering part of the job ↔ By making it part of the job.\n过去式陈述改写成 By doing，it 指代志愿\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 By linking it with employees' salary. 借 employees 这个录音高频词显得耳熟，但录\n音从未说把志愿与‘薪水’挂钩，salary 是凭常识脑补的合理做法。\n借用原文：employees and employers would actually benefit\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音确有 company volunteer programs could be the perfect 2025 resolution，本项\nBy taking it as the New Year resolution. 抠走 resolution 一词，但那只是把项目比作‘新\n年愿望’的修辞，不是‘如何让双方受益’的办法，答非所问。\n借用原文：company volunteer programs could be the perfect 2025 resolution\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音 Most people cite time and conflicting work schedules as major barriers 把 work\nschedules 说成‘障碍’，本项 By improving work schedules. 把它翻成‘改善作息’当对策，\n正是 but 转折前的干扰内容，与录音给的真正办法不符。\n借用原文：conflicting work schedules as major barriers", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-2-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "What did 75% of employees say about volunteering in a survey?", "options": ["A) It was very important to their well-being.", "B) It had taken little of their spare time.", "C) It was an outlet for their personal fulfillment.", "D) It helped improve their working environment."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It was very important to their well-being.\n【定位】In one survey, 89% of employees perceived companies that sponsored volunteer activities\nas creating a better working environment, and 75% said volunteering was critical to their\nwell-being.\n在一项调查中，89% 的员工认为赞助志愿活动的公司营造了更好的工作环境，75% 的员工\n则表示志愿服务对他们的幸福感至关重要。\n【信号】数字 75% 直接领出答案，须与相邻 89% 切开。录音连说两个数字，75% said\nvolunteering was critical to their well-being 才是题干要的 75%；听到 75% said 就锁定\n它后半句，切勿被前面 89% 那句串台。\n【替换】critical to their well-being ↔ It was very important to their well-being.\ncritical 同义替换为 very important\n【排除】B) 未提及\n本项 It had taken little of their spare time. 说志愿‘几乎没占业余时间’，录音 75% 的人说\n的是它‘至关重要’，方向不符；录音只在前文把 time 说成‘障碍’，从没说它‘占用很少’。\nC) 张冠李戴\n录音确有 the workplace becomes an outlet for personal fulfillment，本项 It was an\noutlet for their personal fulfillment. 几乎照搬这句，但那是作者自己的论述，不是 75%\n员工‘在调查中说’的内容，张冠李戴。\n借用原文：the workplace becomes an outlet for personal fulfillment\nD) 张冠李戴\nIt helped improve their working environment. 取自 89% of employees perceived\ncompanies…as creating a better working environment，是相邻的 89% 那句的内容；\n题干问 75%，本项把另一个数字的说法搬过来，是典型数字串台。\n借用原文：89% of employees perceived companies that sponsored volunteer activities as creating\na better working environment", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-2-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "What do we learn about the UK's retail sector food waste?", "options": ["A) It amounts to 2,000 tons daily.", "B) It mostly comes from supermarkets.", "C) It is estimated at 11% of the UK's total food supply.", "D) It has a lot to do with independent luxury food suppliers."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It mostly comes from supermarkets.\n【定位】The vast majority of the UK's 200,000 tonnes of retail sector's annual food waste comes\nfrom supermarkets.\n英国零售业每年 20 万吨的食物浪费，绝大部分来自超市。\n【信号】首句正面定性 the vast majority。这是段落开篇的总起句，用 The vast majority（绝大部\n分）直接给 \"来源\" 下定性结论——细节题的答案常落在这种正面交代主体的句子上，而不\n是后面的补充。\n【替换】The vast majority of the UK's 200,000 tonnes of retail sector's annual food waste comes from\nsupermarkets\n↔ It mostly comes from supermarkets.\nthe vast majority = mostly\n【排除】A) 未提及\n录音只说全年总量是 200,000 tonnes（20 万吨/年），选项 It amounts to 2,000 tons\ndaily. 把数字改成 \"2,000 吨/天\"，数额和 \"每日\" 的口径都是录音没说的，属于拿一个貌似\n听过的数字来骗。\n借用原文：200,000 tonnes\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音说的是 It's estimated at less than one per cent of the UK's food waste total\n——\"不到 1%，且是占食物浪费总量\"。选项 It is estimated at 11% of the UK's total\nfood supply. 盗用了 \"estimated\" 这个原词，却把 \"less than one per cent\" 改成\n\"11%\"、把 \"food waste total\" 改成 \"food supply\"，数字与口径全反了。\n借用原文：It's estimated at less than one per cent of the UK's food waste total\nD) 张冠李戴\n录音说浪费主要来自超市，只是 it's a problem that impacts independent luxury food\nsuppliers too——高端供应商只是 \"也受影响\" 的次要对象。选项 It has a lot to do with\nindependent luxury food suppliers. 盗用 \"independent luxury food suppliers\" 这串原\n词，把次要对象说成主因，张冠李戴。\n借用原文：it's a problem that impacts independent luxury food suppliers too", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-2-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "What does Sophie André's business do in London?", "options": ["A) It rents bicycles to deliver food.", "B) It sells sought-after coffee beans.", "C) It delivers luxury hand-made food.", "D) It recycles waste food from the city."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It delivers luxury hand-made food.\n【定位】In 2016 she moved to London and began by setting up a business delivering luxury hand-\nmade food across the city.\n2016 年她搬到伦敦，先创办了一家在全城配送高端手工食品的公司。\n【信号】creating/setting up 引出业务定义。began by setting up a business delivering luxury\nhand-made food 用 \"setting up a business doing…\" 的句式直接给这家公司的主营下定\n义——这种 \"创办一家做……的公司\" 句就是业务题的答案句。\n【替换】setting up a business delivering luxury hand-made food across the city ↔\nIt delivers luxury hand-made food.\n几近原词的定性概括\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音里 \"自行车\" 出现在 I started delivering products by bicycle——自行车只是配送 \"工\n具\"。选项 It rents bicycles to deliver food. 盗用 \"bicycle\" 原词，却把 \"用自行车送货\" 偷\n换成 \"出租自行车\"，业务定位完全错了。\n借用原文：I started delivering products by bicycle\nB) 原词陷阱\n\"coffee beans\" 出现在第一段 sought-after coffee beans gets thrown away，说的是 \"被\n浪费的高端食材举例\"，根本不是这家公司在卖的东西。选项 It sells sought-after coffee\nbeans. 盗用这串原词，安到公司业务上，答非所问。\n借用原文：sought-after coffee beans gets thrown away every day\nD) 与原文相反\n录音明确说这家公司 the idea is not simply to redistribute waste food, but to provide\na high-quality product——明确否定了 \"只是重新分配废弃食物\"。选项 It recycles waste\nfood from the city. 盗用 \"waste\" 原词，恰好把录音否定掉的那层意思当成业务，与原文相\n反。\n借用原文：the idea is not simply to redistribute waste food, but to provide a high-quality product", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-2-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "In what way does Sophie André's business differ from many food waste projects?", "options": ["A) It pays special attention to the size and shape of its products.", "B) It tries to escape the constraints of the current food system.", "C) It cooperates closely with local producers.", "D) It aims to provide high-quality products."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It aims to provide high-quality products.\n【定位】Unlike many food waste projects that have sprung up in recent years, the idea is not simply to\nredistribute waste food, but to provide a high-quality product.\n与近年涌现的许多食物浪费项目不同，这个想法不只是重新分配废弃食物，而是提供高品质\n产品。\n【信号】Unlike + not…but 双重对比。这句同时挂着两个信号：开头 Unlike many food waste\nprojects 是明示对比，中间 not simply…but… 把真正不同之处推到 but 之后——答案句就\n是这种 \"对比词 + 转折落点\" 叠加的句子。\n【替换】to provide a high-quality product ↔ It aims to provide high-quality products.\nbut 后落点的同义保留\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n\"size or shape\" 出现在 a small part of the production is sometimes wasted due to\nproblems with size or shape——是在解释 \"为何会有剩余\"，不是公司的产品特点。选项\nIt pays special attention to the size and shape of its products. 盗用这两个词，编出 \"特\n别关注尺寸形状\" 这个录音没说的差异。\n借用原文：wasted due to problems with size or shape\nB) 过度推断\n录音只说它 \"不只是再分配废弃食物，而是提供高品质产品\"，并没说它要 \"摆脱现有食物体\n系的束缚\"。选项 It tries to escape the constraints of the current food system. 把朴素\n的 \"提供高品质\" 拔高成宏大的体制叙事，是录音支撑不了的引申。\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音只说她 Talking with local producers I learned that…——只是 \"和本地生产者交谈\"\n从而得知信息，并未说 \"紧密合作\"。选项 It cooperates closely with local producers. 盗\n用 \"local producers\" 原词，把 \"交谈\" 夸大成 \"closely cooperate\"，且这也不是题问的差\n异点。\n借用原文：Talking with local producers I learned that…", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-2-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "Why will suppliers always have a certain amount of surplus?", "options": ["A) To ensure a regular supply.", "B) To maintain a unique menu.", "C) To make up for the waste products.", "D) To meet customers' urgent demand."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） To ensure a regular supply.\n【定位】Perhaps unlike other waste food, the supply is regular. Suppliers will always have a certain\namount of surplus. So the menus remain regular too.\n也许与其他剩余食物不同，这里的供应是稳定的。供应商总会有一定量的剩余。所以菜单也\n保持稳定。\n【信号】the supply is regular 的因果链。这题的因果不在 \"surplus\" 那句本身，而在它前面的 the\nsupply is regular——正因要保证 \"稳定供应\"，所以供应商总会留出一定剩余；\"surplus\"\n那句与 \"so the menus remain regular\" 共同把 \"regular（稳定）\" 这条逻辑钉死。\n【替换】the supply is regular ↔ To ensure a regular supply.\nregular supply 几乎原词对应\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n录音说的是 So the menus remain regular too——菜单 \"保持稳定（regular）\"。选项 To\nmaintain a unique menu. 盗用 \"menu\" 原词，却把 \"regular（稳定）\" 偷换成 \"unique\n（独特）\"，把听过的词配上录音没说的形容词。\n借用原文：So the menus remain regular too\nC) 原词陷阱\n\"waste products\" 出现在 Elysia uses waste products to create…meals——是说公司 \"用\n剩余食材做餐\"，并非 \"供应商留剩余是为弥补废弃\"。选项 To make up for the waste\nproducts. 盗用 \"waste products\" 这个主题原词，编出一个录音没有的因果，答非所问。\n借用原文：Elysia uses waste products to create \"simple, natural, straightforward\" meals and snacks\nD) 未提及\n录音通篇没说 \"满足顾客的紧急需求\"，只讲了供应稳定、菜单稳定。选项 To meet\ncustomers' urgent demand. 里的 \"urgent demand\" 是录音完全没出现的内容，属凭空捏\n造。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-2-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) caught", "B) cinema", "C) engaging", "D) graphically", "E) highly", "F) incentive", "G) instincts", "H) offences", "I) punished", "J) referred", "K) stood", "L) symbolic", "M) tuned", "N) visually", "O) worshipped"], "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J) referred\n动词\n【词性槽】Famously ___ to as 是过去分词作状语\n【依据】be referred to as 是\"被称为\"的固定搭配\nFamously __(26)__ to as 'The Master of Suspense' 中 to as 紧贴空后，这是经典短语\nrefer to A as B = 把 A 称作 B 的被动式 be referred to as = 被称为。代入即 \"以\"悬疑\n大师\"之名广为人知\"，与后文 one of the most influential filmmakers 的赞誉口吻完全\n一致。\n【竞争词】A) caught 搭配不成立\ncatch 不和 to as 搭配；caught to as 在英语里不存在这个固定结构。\nI) punished 语义反\n\"惩罚\" 与后文盛赞他是大师的褒义基调完全相反，而且 punished to as 也不是固定搭配。\nK) stood 搭配不通\nstand to as 不构成习语；stood 本卷另有专属搭配 stand the test of time，留给 34 题更\n合适。\nM) tuned 义不沾边\ntune 表\"调音/调频\"，和\"被称为\"风马牛不相及，也不与 to as 搭配。\nO) worshipped 搭配错位\nworship 表\"崇拜\"，但 worshipped to as 不是英语搭配；崇拜在第 35 题位置语义更贴。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2025-06-2-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) caught", "B) cinema", "C) engaging", "D) graphically", "E) highly", "F) incentive", "G) instincts", "H) offences", "I) punished", "J) referred", "K) stood", "L) symbolic", "M) tuned", "N) visually", "O) worshipped"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) cinema\n名词\n【词性槽】the history of ___ 介词宾语位\n【依据】话题是电影业的历史\n上文已说他是 an English movie director and producer，又称他 one of the most\ninfluential filmmakers in the history of __(27)__，话题主线是\"电影行业\"，filmmakers\nin the history of cinema = 电影史上最有影响力的导演之一，是地道表达。\n【竞争词】F) incentive 义不沾边\nincentive 指\"激励/动机\"，与 history of 搭配后意思变成\"激励史\"，与上下文电影主题脱节。\nG) instincts 话题错位\ninstincts(本能)与电影导演的历史无关；history of instincts 没有意义。\nH) offences 话题错位\noffences(犯罪)在第 31 题的电影题材列表里更合适，这里讲行业地位、不是讲犯罪史。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2025-06-2-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) caught", "B) cinema", "C) engaging", "D) graphically", "E) highly", "F) incentive", "G) instincts", "H) offences", "I) punished", "J) referred", "K) stood", "L) symbolic", "M) tuned", "N) visually", "O) worshipped"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) engaging\n形容词\n【词性槽】with ___ and gripping suspense movies 形容词作定语\n【依据】与 gripping 同义并列，描述电影吸引人\ngripping = 扣人心弦的，与 engaging = 引人入胜的 属同义并列，二者都是描述电影本身\n的积极特质，与 entertained his audience 的动词\"使观众尽兴\"高度自洽。\n【竞争词】L) symbolic 语义错位\nsymbolic = 象征性的，描述\"代表某种意义\"的属性，不直接形容电影对观众的吸引力，与\ngripping 不构成同义并列；symbolic 更贴 30 题 be symbolic of 的死搭配。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2025-06-2-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) caught", "B) cinema", "C) engaging", "D) graphically", "E) highly", "F) incentive", "G) instincts", "H) offences", "I) punished", "J) referred", "K) stood", "L) symbolic", "M) tuned", "N) visually", "O) worshipped"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) punished\n动词\n【词性槽】was ___ by his father 被动语态\n【依据】因小过失被父亲惩罚\n句中 made him spend a few minutes inside a prison for his misconduct = 因为他不当\n行为而让他在监狱里待几分钟，明确是父亲对他施加的**惩罚**性教训，be punished by\n完全贴合。\n【竞争词】A) caught 义偏\nbe caught by 意为\"被……抓到\"，但下文重点是父亲让他蹲监狱的\"教训\"行为，不是\"抓住\"这一\n动作；且本句末为 for his misconduct，更指向惩罚因果。\nK) stood 搭配错\nstand 不能这样被动，且与上下文教育/惩罚的主题无关。\nM) tuned 义不沾边\ntune 是调音/调试，与父亲管教孩子毫无逻辑联系。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2025-06-2-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) caught", "B) cinema", "C) engaging", "D) graphically", "E) highly", "F) incentive", "G) instincts", "H) offences", "I) punished", "J) referred", "K) stood", "L) symbolic", "M) tuned", "N) visually", "O) worshipped"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) symbolic\n形容词\n【词性槽】are ___ of the guilt 系表结构\n【依据】be symbolic of 表象征着\nbe symbolic of = 象征着、代表着，与 the guilt and innocence of the wrongdoer and\nthe victim 形成的抽象主题(罪与无辜/施害者与受害者)高度匹配——希区柯克的电影在象征\n意义上探讨这些命题。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2025-06-2-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) caught", "B) cinema", "C) engaging", "D) graphically", "E) highly", "F) incentive", "G) instincts", "H) offences", "I) punished", "J) referred", "K) stood", "L) symbolic", "M) tuned", "N) visually", "O) worshipped"], "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H) offences\n名词\n【词性槽】other criminal ___ 形容词修饰的并列名词\n【依据】criminal offences 是固定搭配\ncriminal offences = 刑事罪行，是英语里高度固定的搭配；同时它正是\ndeceit/robbery/murder/corruption 这一串犯罪类名词的总括，逻辑完全闭环。\n【竞争词】F) incentive 义反\nincentive = 激励/动机，是褒义或中性，与\"犯罪\"语境冲突；且 criminal incentive 不是固定\n搭配。\nG) instincts 范畴不同\ninstincts = 本能/直觉，是心理/生物层面，不是\"行为/罪行\"范畴，无法与 deceit/robbery 等\n并列。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 G) —— 范畴近义但维度不同的 instincts。other criminal __(31)__\n里 G instincts 词性、与 criminal 的字面搭配感都没问题，criminal instincts 在日常英语中\n也是常听到的短语，很容易让考生误选。防坑口诀：看 and other 前的并列项决定范畴\n——并列项是\"行为类\"罪行，就必须填\"行为类\"名词 offences，不是\"心理类\"的 instincts。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2025-06-2-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) caught", "B) cinema", "C) engaging", "D) graphically", "E) highly", "F) incentive", "G) instincts", "H) offences", "I) punished", "J) referred", "K) stood", "L) symbolic", "M) tuned", "N) visually", "O) worshipped"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) caught\n动词\n【词性槽】ordinary people ___ in… 后置定语\n【依据】be caught in 表\"陷入……处境\"\nbe caught in + 情境名词 是固定搭配，意为\"被困在/陷入……中\"。in unwanted and\nunavoidable situations 译作\"困于自己不想要、也无法回避的境地\"，正是希区柯克电影里\n普通人卷入险境的典型情节。\n【竞争词】K) stood 搭配错\nstood in 多指\"站在……里\"或\"代替……\"，无\"陷入境地\"之义；且 K 是 34 题的死搭配。\nM) tuned 义不沾边\ntuned in 多指\"调到某频道/有所了解\"，与处境无关。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2025-06-2-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) caught", "B) cinema", "C) engaging", "D) graphically", "E) highly", "F) incentive", "G) instincts", "H) offences", "I) punished", "J) referred", "K) stood", "L) symbolic", "M) tuned", "N) visually", "O) worshipped"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) highly\n副词\n【词性槽】He was ___ productive 副词修饰形容词\n【依据】highly + 形容词表程度强烈\nhighly productive 是英语里极高频的搭配，表\"高度多产/极为多产\"，与后文 his amazing\nbody of work is considered fascinating 即\"作品体量庞大且令人惊叹\"完全自洽。\n【竞争词】D) graphically 搭配错位\ngraphically(生动地)多修饰描述类动词(graphically describe)，不与 productive 搭配；与\"多\n产\"维度不匹配。\nN) visually 维度错\nvisually(视觉上)是修饰视觉相关属性的(visually stunning)，与 productive(多产)语义维度\n无关。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 N) —— 副词同位置易混淆 visually 与 highly。He was __(33)__\nproductive 三个副词 D、E、N 都能套进副词位，N visually 还容易让考生联想到电影\n是\"视觉艺术\"而误填。防坑口诀：副词修饰形容词时先想\"___ + 形容词\"的高频搭档，highly\nproductive 是地道搭配、visually productive 不是。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2025-06-2-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) caught", "B) cinema", "C) engaging", "D) graphically", "E) highly", "F) incentive", "G) instincts", "H) offences", "I) punished", "J) referred", "K) stood", "L) symbolic", "M) tuned", "N) visually", "O) worshipped"], "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K) stood\n动词\n【词性槽】have ___ the test of time 完成时谓语\n【依据】stand the test of time 经受住时间考验\nstand the test of time 是英语里\"经得起时间考验/历久弥新\"的死搭配，与下文 are\nconsidered to be masterpieces 完全呼应——只有经得起时间考验的作品才会被视为\n杰作。\n【竞争词】M) tuned 搭配不成立\ntune the test of time 在英语里不存在，tune 与\"时间考验\"无搭配。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2025-06-2-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) caught", "B) cinema", "C) engaging", "D) graphically", "E) highly", "F) incentive", "G) instincts", "H) offences", "I) punished", "J) referred", "K) stood", "L) symbolic", "M) tuned", "N) visually", "O) worshipped"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) worshipped\n动词\n【词性槽】is also ___ by people 被动语态\n【依据】全球敬仰与娱乐故事的赞誉\nby people around the world for his entertaining and exciting stories 给出的是\"全世界\n因他精彩的故事而……他\"，褒义、强烈情感，worshipped(被敬仰、被崇拜)与上文 he\ncontinues to inspire them to be passionate about their work 的盛赞口吻一脉相承。\n【竞争词】A) caught 义反\nbe caught by 多指\"被……抓住/逮住\"，含贬义或被动遭遇，与 \"for his entertaining stories\"\n的褒义原因状语冲突。\nI) punished 语义反\n\"被惩罚\" 与全球观众因他的精彩故事而……完全反向，逻辑矛盾。\nK) stood 搭配不通\nstand 是不及物动词，没有 \"is stood by\" 的被动用法；stood 在 34 题与 the test of time 是死\n搭配。\nM) tuned 义不沾边\nbe tuned by 指\"被调音/被调试\"，话题与本句\"被全球观众喜爱\"完全不接。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2025-06-2-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "Fruit juice is different from whole fruit in that it contains much less fibre.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】果汁与整果在纤维含量上的差异\n【定位】Unlike whole fruit, fruit juice has most of the fibre removed.\n与整果不同，果汁中绝大部分纤维已被去除。\n【改写】Fruit juice is different from whole fruit ↔ Unlike whole fruit, fruit juice\ndifferent from 与 Unlike 是同义\nit contains much less fibre ↔ has most of the fibre removed\n含量少 用 被移除 反向表达\n【辨邻】 B B 段虽出现 the fibre found in whole fruits is intact，但角度是 整果纤维完好，并未\n直接陈述果汁的纤维去除，与题干 Fruit juice ... contains much less fibre 的主语不\n一致。\nthe fibre found in whole fruits is intact\nD D 段重心是 Diets high in fibre have been linked to a lower risk of developing\nheart disease，而非陈述 Fruit juice is different from whole fruit 这一对比关系。\nthey still lack valuable fibre", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-06-2-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "Some people believe a juice diet helps to rid our body of harmful or poisonous substances.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】果汁排毒说法\n【定位】Another reason for fruit juice's recent popularity is the argument that it can help to remove\npoisonous or harmful substances from the body.\n果汁近期流行的另一原因是有说法称它能帮助清除体内的有毒或有害物质。\n【改写】Some people believe ↔ the argument that\n信念视角的不同表达\na juice diet helps to rid our body of harmful or poisonous substances ↔\nit can help to remove poisonous or harmful substances from the body\nrid 与 remove 同义\n【辨邻】 K K 段说的是 fruit juice is far from a quick fix，与排毒说法无关，仅在 果汁被高估 的\n大话题上相邻。\nfruit juice is far from a quick fix", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-06-2-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "A lot of research comes to the same conclusion that fruit juice does harm to the human body when it leads to surplus calories.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】多项研究指向热量过剩时果汁有害\n【定位】Aside from the link to type 2 diabetes, many studies agree that fruit juice is harmful if it\ncontributes to an excess in calories.\n除了与二型糖尿病的关联之外，许多研究一致认为，当果汁导致热量过剩时它就是有害的。\n【改写】A lot of research comes to the same conclusion ↔ many studies agree\n得出相同结论 与 一致同意\nfruit juice does harm to the human body ↔ fruit juice is harmful\n动词短语 与 形容词谓语\nwhen it leads to surplus calories ↔ if it contributes to an excess in calories\nleads to 与 contributes to 同义；surplus 与 excess 同义\n【辨邻】 C C 段研究指向的是 fruit juice consumption was linked with increased risk of type\n2 diabetes，而不是 fruit juice does harm to the human body when it leads to\nsurplus calories 这一热量框架。\nfruit juice consumption was linked with increased risk of type 2 diabetes\nF F 段重点是 when they don't lead to over-consumption of calories 时 may have\nsome small benefits，与题干 does harm ... when it leads to surplus calories 方向\n相反。\nfoods containing fructose may have some small benefits for long-term blood sugar control when\nthey don't lead to over-consumption of calories", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-06-2-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "The claimed health benefits of fruit and vegetable juices have rendered the industry very profitable.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】健康宣称使果汁行业利润丰厚\n【定位】Fruit juice also has been linked to claims that it can help you both lose weight and stay\nhealthy. All of which, of course, has made it highly profitable.\n果汁还被宣称能帮助你既减重又保健。所有这些当然让它非常有利可图。\n【改写】The claimed health benefits of fruit and vegetable juices ↔\nclaims that it can help you both lose weight and stay healthy\nclaims 名词化 + 健康好处的展开\nhave rendered the industry very profitable ↔ has made it highly profitable\nrender 与 make 同义", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-06-2-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "To increase the benefits of fruit juice to our health, we'd better choose ripe fruit to make juice.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】选成熟果实榨汁\n【定位】One way we can improve the effects of fruit juice on our health is making sure we select ripe\nfruit to retain as much of its goodness as possible, according to Roger Clemens, professor of\npharmaceutical(药物的) sciences at the University of Southern California.\n南加州大学药物科学教授罗杰·克莱门斯指出，改善果汁对健康影响的一种方法，是确保挑选\n成熟的水果，以尽可能保留它的营养。\n【改写】To increase the benefits of fruit juice to our health ↔\nimprove the effects of fruit juice on our health\n目的状语同义改写\nchoose ripe fruit to make juice ↔ select ripe fruit to retain as much of its goodness as possible\nchoose 与 select 同义；动作目的延展\n【辨邻】 J J 段说的是 There are a lot of nutrients contained in the parts of the fruits, such\nas in apple skins, that are excluded when you juice them，重心是被丢掉的营养，\n并非 choose ripe fruit 这一选材建议。\nnutrients contained in the parts of the fruits, such as in apple skins, that are excluded when you\njuice them", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-06-2-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "Diets containing large amounts of fibre reduce the risk of heart disease, diabetes and other health hazards.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】高纤维饮食预防多种疾病\n【定位】Diets high in fibre have been linked to a lower risk of developing heart disease, stroke, high\nblood pressure and diabetes, and it's recommended adults consume 30g per day.\n高纤维饮食与降低心脏病、中风、高血压和糖尿病的发病风险相关，并建议成年人每天摄入\n30 克纤维。\n【改写】Diets containing large amounts of fibre ↔ Diets high in fibre\nhigh in 与 containing large amounts of 同义\nreduce the risk of heart disease, diabetes and other health hazards ↔\nlinked to a lower risk of developing heart disease, stroke, high blood pressure and diabetes\n降低风险 + 并列项部分列举加兜底\n【辨邻】 B B 段说的是 the fibre found in whole fruits is intact，重心是细胞与果糖的关系，未\n涉及 reduce the risk of heart disease, diabetes 这种多病种预防结论。\nthe fibre found in whole fruits is intact\nC C 段重心是 fruit juice consumption was linked with increased risk of type 2\ndiabetes，是增加而非降低风险，与题干 reduce the risk 方向相反。\nfruit juice consumption was linked with increased risk of type 2 diabetes", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-06-2-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "According to a research paper, drinking fruit juice is beneficial on condition that it is consumed in reasonable amounts.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】适量饮用条件下果汁有益\n【定位】Sievenpiper concludes that the recommended 150ml of fruit juice per day, which is an\naverage serving, is reasonable.\nSievenpiper 得出结论：每天推荐摄入 150 毫升果汁，作为平均一份的量，是合理的。\n【改写】According to a research paper ↔ The paper concluded\n研究论文与该论文同义\ndrinking fruit juice is beneficial ↔ foods containing fructose may have some small benefits\n有益 与 有一些小益处同义\non condition that it is consumed in reasonable amounts ↔\nwhen they don't lead to over-consumption of calories\n适量 的反向同义表达\n【辨邻】 E E 段重心是 He compared studies looking into the effects of fructose-containing\nsugars with control diets free from or lower in these sugars，是研究框架的介\n绍，结论尚未出现，与 According to a research paper 的 结论 视角不匹配。\nTo isolate the effects of consuming too many calories from the effects of foods containing\ndifferent sugars\nC C 段重心是 fruit juice consumption was linked with increased risk of type 2\ndiabetes，与 drinking fruit juice is beneficial 方向相反。\nfruit juice consumption was linked with increased risk of type 2 diabetes", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-06-2-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "One expert claims that juicing wastes a lot of nutrients contained in fruits.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】榨汁浪费水果营养\n【定位】There are a lot of nutrients contained in the parts of the fruits, such as in apple skins, that are\nexcluded when you juice them.\n水果的某些部位（比如苹果皮）含有大量营养，但榨汁时这些营养就被排除掉了。\n【改写】One expert claims that ↔ Heather Ferris, a professor of medicine\n具体专家被泛化为一位专家\njuicing wastes a lot of nutrients contained in fruits ↔\na lot of nutrients contained in the parts of the fruits, such as in apple skins, that are excluded\nwhen you juice them\n排除等于浪费\n【辨邻】 H H 段说的是 making sure we select ripe fruit to retain as much of its goodness as\npossible，是 留住营养 的正面建议，与题干 juicing wastes a lot of nutrients 的负面\n定性相反。\nmaking sure we select ripe fruit to retain as much of its goodness as possible", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-06-2-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "It takes some time for fruit's cells to be digested and for fructose to be absorbed.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】细胞需时间被消化、果糖被吸收\n【定位】It takes our digestive system a while to break these cells down and for fructose to enter\nthe bloodstream.\n我们的消化系统需要一段时间来分解这些细胞，果糖也需要一段时间才能进入血液。\n【改写】It takes some time ↔ It takes our digestive system a while\nsome time 与 a while 同义\nfor fruit's cells to be digested ↔ to break these cells down\n主动改写为被动\nfor fructose to be absorbed ↔ for fructose to enter the bloodstream\n被吸收 等于 进入血液\n【辨邻】 C C 段说的是 fruit juice's fructose is absorbed more quickly，是 快吸收 的对照视\n角，与题干 takes some time ... for fructose to be absorbed 的 需时间 方向相反。\nfruit juice's fructose is absorbed more quickly", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-06-2-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "A lot of research has demonstrated that drinking fruit juice does not reduce our daily consumption of other foods.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】果汁不减少其他食物摄入\n【定位】Numerous studies have shown that drinking fruit juice doesn't make us consume any less of\nother foods throughout the day.\n众多研究表明，喝果汁并不会让我们一整天中减少摄入其他食物。\n【改写】A lot of research has demonstrated ↔ Numerous studies have shown\n研究表明 的两种写法\ndrinking fruit juice does not reduce our daily consumption of other foods ↔\ndrinking fruit juice doesn't make us consume any less of other foods throughout the day\n不减少 等于 不让我们少吃\n【辨邻】 H H 段说的是 As it is easy to consume large amounts of fruit juice quite quickly,\nextra calories are taken in，是 果汁带来额外热量 的视角，与题干 does not reduce\nour daily consumption of other foods 的 不减少其他食物 视角不同。\nAs it is easy to consume large amounts of fruit juice quite quickly, extra calories are taken in", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-06-2-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What does science suggest the right dress may do?", "options": ["A) Add to our advantage in work and life.", "B) Enable us to look a lot more attractive.", "C) Help us to enjoy a fuller personal life.", "D) Provide extra energy for what we do."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What does science suggest the right dress may do? 直接问 science 提出 dress 会\n做什么，属于针对原文某句客观事实的复述。\n【拆句】主干 What does science suggest the right dress may do? 是宾语从句嵌套：science 后\n跟省略 that 的宾语从句 the right dress may do What；理解上把 What 还原到从句末\n尾，即可看清问的是 dress 的功能/作用。\n【定位】Science now suggests the right dress may give ourselves the extra edge in our professional\nand personal lives.\n科学现在表明，穿对衣服可以在我们的职业与生活中给予我们额外的优势。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nthe extra edge ↔ Add to our advantage\nin our professional and personal lives ↔ in work and life\n选项 Add to our advantage in work and life. 恰好覆盖「在职业与生活中增加优\n势」，与预判完全吻合。\nB) 偷换概念\n原文谈的是 the extra edge（优势），选项 Enable us to look a lot more attractive.\n把它换成了外貌吸引力，方向不同。\nC) 以偏概全\n原文同时强调 professional and personal lives 两方面，选项 Help us to enjoy a\nfuller personal life. 只保留 personal life，丢掉了职业一面。\nD) 偷换概念\n原文是 the extra edge（优势），选项 Provide extra energy for what we do. 把\nedge 换成 energy（精力），属于熟词僻义误读。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-2-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "Why does science say the clothes one wears may affect their interaction with others?", "options": ["A) Clothes usually represent one's social and economic status.", "B) Clothes largely determine one's likability by people around.", "C) Different types of clothing markedly reflect different personalities.", "D) Different types of clothing convey different messages symbolically."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干 Why does science say the clothes one wears may affect their interaction with\nothers? 问的是原因，原文应有 because/due to 等显式因果标志。\n【拆句】主干为 Why does science say … ；say 后是省略 that 的宾语从句 the clothes one wears\nmay affect their interaction with others，其中 one wears 是修饰 clothes 的省略关系代\n词的定语从句，理解为「人们穿的衣服」。\n【定位】Science says that the clothes we wear affect our behavior, our mood and even the way\nwe interact with others because of the symbolic meaning that we assign to different types\nof clothing.\n科学表明，我们所穿的衣服会影响我们的行为、情绪甚至我们与他人互动的方式，这是因为\n我们赋予不同类型衣物的象征意义。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n原文从未提及 social and economic status，Clothes usually represent one's social\nand economic status. 是凭印象添加。\nB) 偷换概念\n原文谈的是衣服带来的象征意义影响互动，选项 Clothes largely determine one's\nlikability by people around. 把它替换为「决定一个人受不受人喜欢」，并加重为\nlargely determine。\nC) 偷换概念\n原文说衣服传递象征意义，选项 Different types of clothing markedly reflect\ndifferent personalities. 把它换成「反映性格」，personalities 是原文没有的概念。\nD) ✓ 正确\nthe symbolic meaning that we assign to different types of clothing ↔\nDifferent types of clothing convey different messages symbolically.\nassign ↔ convey\n选项 Different types of clothing convey different messages symbolically. 把原文因\n果端逐一对应，是同义复述。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-2-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "How do the clothes we wear sway our evaluation of ourselves?", "options": ["A) By exerting an effect on our power of judgment.", "B) By impacting how we feel about ourselves.", "C) By affecting what we take as the basis for assessment.", "D) By influencing our interpretation of symbolic messages."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】题干 How do the clothes we wear sway our evaluation of ourselves? 问的是「通过什\n么方式」，答案对应原文一句解释机制的话。\n【拆句】题干主干 the clothes … sway our evaluation of ourselves；the clothes 后省略了 that，\n跟着定语从句 we wear，整体读作「我们所穿的衣服如何影响我们对自己的评价」。\n【定位】We also evaluate ourselves based on what we are wearing because of the way they make\nus feel.\n我们也会基于所穿衣服来评价自己，因为衣服会影响我们的感受。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\n原文 the way they make us feel 谈的是 feel（感受），选项 By exerting an effect on\nour power of judgment. 换成了 power of judgment（判断力），概念发生偏移。\nB) ✓ 正确\nthe way they make us feel ↔ how we feel about ourselves\nbecause of ↔ By impacting\n选项 By impacting how we feel about ourselves. 把原文 the way they make us feel\n用 how we feel about ourselves 改写，完全贴合预判。\nC) 答非所问\n选项 By affecting what we take as the basis for assessment. 描述的是「影响我们用\n什么作为评判依据」，等于反过来在说「clothes 改变了我们的评判标准」，可原文只说\nclothes 让我们对自己有感受，再据此评价自己，并未谈「评判标准本身」。\nD) 张冠李戴\nsymbolic messages 是第 2 段谈衣服影响互动的原因，选项 By influencing our\ninterpretation of symbolic messages. 把它挪到了第 3 段评价自我的机制上，张冠\n李戴。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-2-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "Why does the author say tailored jackets are associated with being 'dressed for success'?", "options": ["A) They are necessary for formal business dealings.", "B) They may help people concentrate on their business.", "C) They are vital to keeping a dominant position in business transactions.", "D) They may enable people to have the right mentality for doing business."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】Why does the author say tailored jackets are associated with being 'dressed for\nsuccess'? 问作者为何这么说，正是细节原因题的典型表述。\n【拆句】主干 Why does the author say + 宾语从句 tailored jackets are associated with being\n'dressed for success'；be associated with 是固定搭配「与……相关」，being 引导的动\n名词短语作介词宾语，整体读作「为什么作者说西装外套与『为成功而着装』相关」。\n【定位】There's a reason tailored jackets are associated with being 'dressed for success'. It seems that\nwearing formal office wear puts us in the right frame of mind to conduct business.\n西装外套之所以被认为与「为成功而着装」相关，是有原因的。看起来，穿正式办公着装会\n让我们进入做生意的正确心理状态。\n【选项】A) 过度推断\n原文只说西装让人进入做生意心态，并未声称它是 necessary（必要条件），They are\nnecessary for formal business dealings. 把「有帮助」拔高为「必备」。\nB) 偷换概念\n原文是 the right frame of mind（正确的心理状态），选项 They may help people\nconcentrate on their business. 把它换成 concentrate（集中注意力），属于偷换。\nC) 过度推断\n原文 Wearing power clothing makes us feel more confident and even increases\nhormones needed for displaying dominance. 是讲附加效果，选项 They are vital to\nkeeping a dominant position in business transactions. 把它拔高为「对保持优势地\n位至关重要」，既偏离了 Q49 问的「为何被称为 dressed for success」，又把程度上\n拉到 vital。\nD) ✓ 正确\nputs us in the right frame of mind ↔ enable people to have the right mentality\nto conduct business ↔ for doing business\n选项 They may enable people to have the right mentality for doing business. 把原\n文「让我们处于做生意的正确心态」逐一对应，是同义复述。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-2-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What are people advised to do when they want to socialize?", "options": ["A) Focus on clothing.", "B) Wear a good suit.", "C) Dress casually.", "D) Look unusual."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What are people advised to do when they want to socialize? 询问具体建议行为，是典\n型的细节复述。\n【拆句】主干 What are people advised to do；when 引导时间状语从句 they want to\nsocialize，整体读作「当人们想社交时，建议穿什么」。被动语态 are advised 隐含「文章\n作者建议」。\n【定位】On the other hand, a casual dress helps us become more friendly and creative. These findings\nsupport the idea of wearing business casuals on a Friday; since colleagues are most likely to\ntake out time to socialize on the last work day of the week.\n另一方面，休闲着装能让我们变得更友好、更有创造力。这些发现支持周五穿商务休闲装的\n做法，因为同事们最有可能在一周的最后一个工作日抽时间社交。\n【选项】A) 答非所问\n原文从未建议把注意力集中在「衣服」本身，Focus on clothing. 与「社交场合该穿什\n么」毫无对应。\nB) 与原文相反\n原文明说 wearing formal wear isn't a great idea when we want to socialize，选项\nWear a good suit. 恰好建议穿西装，与原文方向相反。\nC) ✓ 正确\na casual dress ↔ Dress casually\nwearing formal wear isn't a great idea when we want to socialize ↔ Dress casually\n选项 Dress casually. 把 a casual dress 的建议浓缩为祈使句，完整对应原文。\nD) 无中生有\n原文从未提到「穿得与众不同」，Look unusual. 是凭空生造的建议。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-2-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What does the author think of classical music and opera in today's world?", "options": ["A) They still make their presence felt.", "B) They have given way to electronic music.", "C) They will not fade away from the public eye.", "D) They are no longer relevant to teenagers' lives."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （态度题）\n【判型】题干 What does the author think of classical music and opera in today's world? 用\nthink of 直接问作者看法，对象又是全文核心话题，是典型的整体态度题。\n【拆句】题干主干是 What does the author think of …，think of sth = 对某事物的看法；in\ntoday's world 是时间地点状语，限定评价的时间范围。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nboth opera and classical music are now still quite alive ↔ They still make their presence felt\n四个选项里只有 They still make their presence felt 与预判「仍有存在感」的肯定立场\n吻合，其余三项要么表否定立场、要么改换主语。\nB) 与原文相反\nP1 只列举 pop music, jazz, and electronic music 作为崛起的对手，没说古典乐和歌\n剧已 give way to（让位给）电子乐。\nC) 无中生有\n原文 started to fade away from the public eye 是已发生的事实，选项 They will not\nfade away from the public eye 把它改成对将来的否定预言，是无中生有的预言。\nD) 以偏概全\nyou will not typically see today's teenagers lending their ears to Bach anytime\nsoon 只是承认青少年不常听，但作者用 Granted（诚然）让步后立刻 but 反转，重点\n不在青少年身上。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "态度题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-2-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What do we learn about much of the popular music currently produced?", "options": ["A) It can be difficult for many classical music fans to appreciate.", "B) It can be seen as an indicator of refinement on classical music.", "C) It caters to society's taste in music in a more subtle way than classical music.", "D) It contains elements similar to those in some masterpieces of classical music."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn about much of the popular music currently produced? 是对某具体\n对象的客观信息提问，不带 infer/imply，按细节题处理。\n【拆句】much of the popular music currently produced 是名词短语：much of 限定范围（大部\n分），currently produced 是过去分词短语作后置定语修饰 popular music = 当下被制作\n出来的流行音乐。后置分词定语是四级长难句高频结构，识别它能避免把 produced 误读成\n谓语。\n【定位】The most major indicator of classical music's importance in society today is the fact that\nmuch of the popular music that is currently being produced uses similar beats, harmonies, and\nmelodies(旋律) as those that were used in some of classical music's best works.\n古典乐在当今社会重要性的最显著标志是：当下被制作出来的大部分流行乐使用了与古典乐\n一些最佳作品相似的节拍、和声与旋律。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\n原文说的是「不学乐理的人难以察觉这一现象」，没说流行乐让古典乐迷难以欣赏。\nB) 过度推断\n原文只说流行乐「使用相似要素」，并未称其是古典乐的 refinement（精炼/提升）。\nC) 张冠李戴\n原文是「交响乐对社会音乐品味的影响是 subtle 的」，主语是交响乐的影响、不是流\n行乐。\nD) ✓ 正确\nuses similar beats, harmonies, and melodies(旋律) as those that were used in some of classical\nmusic's best works\n↔ It contains elements similar to those in some masterpieces of classical music\n选项 D 把 similar 与 some of classical music's best works 都原样保留，仅把并列名\n词上位为 elements，是定位句的标准同义替换。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-2-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "Why does the author mention the invention of the rock opera?", "options": ["A) To illustrate how to tell a story using music.", "B) To present the simplest definition of opera.", "C) To show the relevance of opera and classical music.", "D) To justify the necessity of using orchestras in opera."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （例证目的题）\n【判型】题干 Why does the author mention the invention of the rock opera? 直接问举例的目\n的，是例证目的题的最典型句式。\n【拆句】题干主干 Why does the author mention X？X = the invention of the rock opera，of\n短语作 the invention 的后置定语。结构简单，无陷阱。\n【定位】A better example for the relevance of opera and classical music can be seen in the invention\nof the rock opera.\n歌剧与古典乐仍具相关性的一个更佳例证，可以从摇滚歌剧的发明中看出。\n【选项】A) 答非所问\nOpera, in its simplest definition, is telling a story using music as its form 只是为了\n说明摇滚歌剧的合理性而插入的歌剧定义，不是举摇滚歌剧的目的。\nB) 以偏概全\n段中确实给了歌剧的最简定义，但那是为论证服务的工具，不是举摇滚歌剧的目的。\nC) ✓ 正确\nA better example for the relevance of opera and classical music can be seen in the invention of\nthe rock opera\n↔ To show the relevance of opera and classical music\n原文已把例子的服务对象 the relevance of opera and classical music 写在前置位置，\n选项 C 用不定式 to show 把这个目的复述出来，是逐字命中。\nD) 过度推断\n原文只说 sometimes actual orchestras are used for major parts of the opera itself\n——是 sometimes（有时），不是 necessity（必要性）。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "例证目的题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-2-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "Why are classical music pieces still used as background music in modern movies?", "options": ["A) There are few types of music for movie producers to choose from.", "B) They are considered to be the most expressive type of music.", "C) They are well received by movie fans from all over the world.", "D) They are essential for movies to become the world's greatest hits."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】题干 Why are classical music pieces still used as background music in modern\nmovies? 是对一个具体事实问原因，答案在原文有直接对应，按细节题处理。\n【拆句】题干主干 Why are classical music pieces still used as background music ...？被动语态\nare used，still 表「仍然」，in modern movies 是地点状语。识别 are used 是被动而非\n主动是关键，能避免把题理解成「古典乐使用了什么」。\n【定位】Fans of classical music can also tell you that there are few types of music that are more\nexpressive. So, it should come as no surprise to anyone that classical music pieces are still\nused as background music in modern movies.\n古典乐迷也会告诉你，没有多少类型的音乐比它更具表现力。所以，古典乐至今仍被用作现\n代电影的背景音乐就一点也不意外了。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n原文从未说「电影制作方可选的音乐类型很少」，only 谈古典乐自身的表现力强。\nB) ✓ 正确\nthere are few types of music that are more expressive ↔\nThey are considered to be the most expressive type of music\n题干问 why，定位句的 So 前那句被替换为最高级表达，正好回答原因；这是唯一与原\n因句对应的选项。\nC) 因果颠倒\n原文 These are often very well received amongst mainstream music fans 是讲电影\n配乐的结果——观众接受——并未说这是它被选作配乐的原因。\nD) 张冠李戴\nSome of the world's greatest hits have been parts of rock operas 谈的是摇滚歌剧\n的成就，与电影配乐被使用的原因无关。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-2-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What do we learn about our modern music?", "options": ["A) It could not have come into being without classical music and opera as its foundation.", "B) It cannot outcompete classical music and opera in its impact on our current society.", "C) It will not enjoy as much popularity as classical music and opera among music fans.", "D) It might not be able to rival classical music and opera in importance for centuries to come."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn about our modern music? 是对 modern music 的客观信息提问，按\n细节题处理；但因对象是全文性概念，定位句多落在尾段总结。\n【拆句】题干主干 What do we learn about our modern music？our modern music 是介词宾\n语，about 引出谈论对象。结构简单，无陷阱。\n【定位】Classical music and opera are the very foundation of what our modern music is based upon.\n古典乐与歌剧正是我们现代音乐所赖以建立的根基。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nClassical music and opera are the very foundation of what our modern music is based upon ↔\nIt could not have come into being without classical music and opera as its foundation\n选项 A 用「否定 + without」的常见同义改写技法，把肯定陈述变成必要条件，逻辑完\n全等价，是定位句标准的同义替换。\nB) 无中生有\n原文从未拿现代音乐和古典乐/歌剧比对社会影响力的高下，仅强调古典乐和歌剧仍\n重要。\nC) 偷换概念\n原文谈的是「根基」与「重要性」，不是受欢迎程度（popularity）的比较。\nD) 过度推断\n原文 we can expect them to continue to remain important for centuries to come\n只是肯定古典乐和歌剧的持久重要，没有否定现代音乐重要性。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}]});
